const e=[{id:1,code:"MIL-101",icon:"Siren",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Rapid response",subtitle:"Rapid emergency response",summary:"Procedures for rapid response during emergency incidents",blocks:[{body:"In military situations, speed of response saves lives. Readiness and continuous training are essential."},{heading:"1. Situation assessment (SALUTE)",body:"Rapid report:",points:["S - Size: number and size.","A - Activity: what they are doing.","L - Location: the place (coordinates).","U - Unit: unit or type.","T - Time: the time.","E - Equipment: equipment and materiel."]},{heading:"2. Fire response",body:"On a military base:",points:["Alert: siren or shouting, and call 115 immediately.","Firefighting team: act quickly, but only on small fires that are away from ammunition and explosives.","Evacuation: in an orderly manner.","Assembly: at the designated assembly point, upwind and outside the danger area.","Headcount: every person must be accounted for."]},{heading:"3. Field first aid",body:"First aid in the field:",points:["Life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg: apply a tourniquet immediately and write down the time it was applied.","Fractures: immobilize the limb and do not move it.","Burns: hold the burned area under cool running water for 20 minutes.","CPR: 30:2 (if you are not trained, chest compressions only), and call 122."]},{heading:"4. The first minutes: scene safety",body:"Before you act, take a quick look at the scene:",points:["Dangers: fire, smoke, leaking fuel, fallen wires, damaged structures or suspicious objects.","Protect yourself first: a rescuer who is injured becomes one more casualty.","Raise the alarm and call 115; if there are casualties, call 122 as well.","Keep everyone else back and send one person to guide the 115 team to the scene.","Do not enter a burning or collapsed building without the right equipment and training."]},{heading:"5. The call to 115 and 122",body:"Speak calmly and give the information in this order:",points:["Location first: the base or road, a landmark and the gate or building.","What happened: fire, collapse, vehicle accident or something else.","How many people are hurt or trapped.","Special dangers: ammunition, fuel, gas or suspicious objects.","Your name and phone number; do not hang up until you are told to."]},{heading:"6. Command, headcount and handover",body:"Order prevents confusion:",points:["One person takes command at the scene until a senior officer or the 115 team takes over.","Give each person one clear task: alarm, first aid, guiding the team or the headcount.","Count everyone at the assembly point; report anyone missing to the 115 team and do not go back in to search.","Brief the arriving team: what happened, what has been done, casualties, dangers and missing persons.","After the incident: review what happened, replace used equipment and update the plan."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_response.mp4",title:"Rapid response",description:"Key procedures"}],quiz:[{question:"In SALUTE, what does the letter L stand for?",options:[{text:"Lighting",correct:!1},{text:"Location",correct:!0},{text:"Length",correct:!1},{text:"List",correct:!1}],explanation:"L = Location: the place and its coordinates."},{question:"On a military base, which fires may the firefighting team tackle?",options:[{text:"Any fire, as long as the team arrives quickly enough",correct:!1},{text:"Fires right next to the ammunition, so that the ammunition is protected",correct:!1},{text:"Any fire, as long as enough extinguishers are available",correct:!1},{text:"Only small fires that are away from ammunition and explosives",correct:!0}],explanation:"The lesson limits the firefighting team to small fires that are away from ammunition and explosives; raise the alert and call 115 at once."},{question:"A soldier has life-threatening bleeding from a leg. What does this lesson tell you to do?",options:[{text:"Apply a tourniquet at once and write down the time it was applied",correct:!0},{text:"Raise the leg and wait to see whether the bleeding stops on its own",correct:!1},{text:"Apply a tourniquet and loosen it every few minutes to let blood through",correct:!1},{text:"Wash the wound with water and leave it uncovered so it can dry",correct:!1}],explanation:"For life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, the lesson says: apply a tourniquet immediately and write down the time it was applied."},{question:"You are the first to reach a vehicle fire inside the base. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Check the scene for danger, raise the alarm and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Run to the vehicle and open the doors to look inside",correct:!1},{text:"Start fighting the fire before telling anyone about it",correct:!1},{text:"Wait at a distance until the commander gives an order",correct:!1}],explanation:"First make sure the scene is safe, raise the alarm and call 115; every minute counts and your own safety comes first."},{question:"After a fire in a barracks, one soldier is missing at the assembly point. What is right?",options:[{text:"Tell the commander and the 115 team where he was last seen",correct:!0},{text:"Go back into the barracks quickly to search for him yourself",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until the fire is out and then look for him inside",correct:!1},{text:"Assume he has left the base and carry on with the headcount",correct:!1}],explanation:"Report the missing person to the commander and the 115 team; going back into a burning building creates more casualties."},{question:"Which information do you give first when you call 115?",options:[{text:"The exact location of the incident",correct:!0},{text:"Your full name and military rank",correct:!1},{text:"What you think caused the fire",correct:!1},{text:"How many vehicles are at the base",correct:!1}],explanation:"Location comes first, so the team can set off even if the call is cut; then what happened, casualties, dangers and your name and number."}],steps:[{title:"Check the scene",detail:"Stop for a moment and look for fire, fuel, wires, damaged structures and suspicious objects. Your own safety comes first.",critical:!0},{title:"Raise the alarm and call",detail:"Use the siren or shout, then call 115, and 122 if there are casualties. Give the location first.",critical:!0},{title:"Move people out of danger",detail:"Evacuate in an orderly way to the assembly point, upwind and outside the danger area.",critical:!0},{title:"Give life-saving first aid",detail:"Life-threatening limb bleeding: tourniquet and note the time. No normal breathing: start CPR and call 122. If the place is safe, do not move a casualty with a suspected neck or back injury.",critical:!0},{title:"Count and report",detail:"Count everyone at the assembly point and report missing persons to the commander and the 115 team.",critical:!0},{title:"Hand over to the 115 team",detail:"Tell them what happened, what was done, the casualties, the dangers and who is missing, then follow their instructions."}],warnings:["Never fight a fire that is near ammunition or explosives: get everyone away and call 115.","Never go back into a burning building to look for someone: tell the 115 team where the person was last seen.","Do not move a casualty with a suspected neck or back injury unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed."],tips:["Know where the alarm points, fire extinguishers, first aid kits and the assembly point are on your base.","Practice the call to 115 with the location first, so it comes out right under stress.","Carry your tourniquet where you can reach it with either hand.","Take part in every drill and discuss afterwards what went well and what must change."],activity:{prompt:"Night shift on a base: a fire starts in a storage tent at the edge of the base, and boxes of ammunition are stacked next to it. You are the first to see it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the nearest extinguisher and fight the fire to protect the ammunition",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the team tackles only small fires away from ammunition; next to ammunition the risk of an explosion is too high."},{text:"I raise the alarm, call 115 and move everyone to the assembly point, upwind and outside the danger area",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm, the call to 115 and an orderly evacuation away from the ammunition protect everyone; then the headcount."},{text:"I carry the ammunition boxes away from the tent before the fire reaches them",correct:!1,feedback:"No: working next to a fire and ammunition puts your life at risk; leave the area and let the specialists decide."},{text:"I stay at my post until an officer arrives and gives an order",correct:!1,feedback:"No: every minute counts; the alarm and the call to 115 must not wait for an order."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A vehicle has hit a generator inside the base. A small fire is burning at the generator, fuel is leaking and the driver is still in his seat, bleeding heavily from his thigh. You are the first to arrive. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I raise the alarm, call 115 and 122 with the location first and keep others back from the fuel and the fire",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: help sets off at once and no one else is put at risk; the base fire team deals with the small fire only if it is safe."},{text:"I pull the driver out by his arms at once and drag him across the ground",correct:!1,feedback:"No: you have not checked the scene, and pulling him may worsen a neck or back injury; move him only if the fire threatens him."},{text:"I start the vehicle and reverse it away from the generator",correct:!1,feedback:"No: engine sparks near leaking fuel can start a larger fire, and moving the vehicle can hurt the driver."},{text:"I take photos of the scene first for the incident report",correct:!1,feedback:"No: photos and reports come after the incident; now the alarm, the calls and safety come first."}]},{prompt:"The base fire team has put out the small fire and the scene is now safe. The driver is awake, still bleeding heavily from his thigh, and says his neck hurts. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I lift his leg up high and wait to see whether the bleeding stops",correct:!1,feedback:"No: raising the leg is not taught for life-threatening bleeding; the tourniquet is needed now."},{text:"I apply a tourniquet above the wound, write down the time, keep him still and warm and wait for 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: bleeding control comes first, and with neck pain he stays where he is as long as the place is safe."},{text:"I roll him into the recovery position so he is more comfortable",correct:!1,feedback:"No: he is awake and has neck pain; the recovery position is not used when a neck or back injury is suspected."},{text:"I help him stand up and walk him to the first aid point",correct:!1,feedback:"No: walking with heavy bleeding and neck pain can make both injuries worse; bring the help to him."}]}],realCase:"Late one evening, a guard on a base saw smoke coming from a kitchen tent next to the fuel store. He sounded the siren, called 115 and gave the location first: the base, the gate and the tent beside the fuel store. The duty officer took command, sent one soldier to the gate to guide the fire engines and led everyone to the assembly point upwind. The headcount showed that a cook was missing. No one went back in; the officer told the arriving 115 team where the cook had last been seen, and the team found him behind the tent with a burned hand. His hand was cooled under running water until the ambulance arrived."},{id:2,code:"MIL-102",icon:"Tent",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Setting up a camp",subtitle:"Safe camp setup",summary:"Setting up a military camp safely",blocks:[{body:"A military camp must be protected from both natural and man-made hazards."},{heading:"1. Site selection",body:"A suitable site:",points:["High ground: not in gullies or valleys (flood risk).","Close to water, but not too close.","Sheltered from the wind, but not under large trees (falling branches and lightning).","Access and exit routes: clear and open."]},{heading:"2. Camp layout",body:"Main areas:",points:["Tents: in rows, with spacing between them.","Fire area: away from the tents.","Latrines (toilets): away from water and food, and downwind.","First aid point: in the center of the camp."]},{heading:"3. Camp safety",body:"Important rules:",points:["Fires: continuous watch (24 hours).","Electricity: generators must be kept away from tents (risk of CO gas).","Fuel: in a dedicated, separate location.","Fire extinguishers: near the tents and fire areas."]},{heading:"4. Fire safety in tents",body:"Tents burn quickly, so:",points:["No smoking, candles or open flames inside tents.","Heaters and cookers only of the type approved by command, away from the tent walls and bedding, and never left burning while people sleep.","Keep space between tents and clear the dry grass around them, so that a fire cannot jump from one tent to the next.","A fire watch at all times and an alarm signal everyone knows (siren or whistle).","Everyone knows where the fire extinguishers are and has practiced using them."]},{heading:"5. Carbon monoxide and fuel",body:"Carbon monoxide (CO) has no color or smell and can kill people in their sleep:",points:["Generators run outdoors only, away from tents; never inside a tent, even with the door open.","Never bring a charcoal grill, fuel stove or generator into a tent for warmth.","Signs of CO poisoning: headache, dizziness, nausea, weakness and confusion; several people in one tent feel ill at the same time.","If CO is suspected: everyone out into fresh air, call 122 from outside and do not go back in until the place is declared safe.","Refuel only with the engine switched off and cool, and keep fuel in its own store away from tents and fire areas."]},{heading:"6. Weather and the camp emergency plan",body:"The camp must be ready before anything happens:",points:["Floods: follow weather warnings; if the water starts rising, move to higher ground at once and never walk or drive into flood water.","Lightning: a tent gives no protection; when thunder roars, go into a solid building or a hard-topped vehicle and stay there until 30 minutes after the last thunder.","Agree an assembly point, routes to it that are kept clear and an alarm signal everyone knows.","Keep a list of who sleeps in which tent, so that the headcount is quick.","Give Civil Defense (115) the camp location and the access route, and follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/mil_camp.png",title:"Camp",description:"Layout"}],quiz:[{question:"Where should the fire area be?",options:[{text:"Near the tents",correct:!1},{text:"Away from the tents",correct:!0},{text:"Inside a tent",correct:!1},{text:"Anywhere in the camp",correct:!1}],explanation:"Away from the tents, so that the fire does not spread to them."},{question:"Why should a camp be set up on high ground and not in gullies or valleys?",options:[{text:"Because high ground is better sheltered from the wind",correct:!1},{text:"Because of the flood risk",correct:!0},{text:"Because it is closer to large trees for shade",correct:!1},{text:"Because valleys are too far from water",correct:!1}],explanation:"The lesson chooses high ground and not gullies or valleys because of the flood risk."},{question:"Where should generators be kept in the camp, and why?",options:[{text:"Inside a tent, to protect them from rain and dust",correct:!1},{text:"Next to the fuel store, so that refueling is quick",correct:!1},{text:"Away from the tents, because of the risk of CO gas",correct:!0},{text:"In the center of the camp, beside the first aid point",correct:!1}],explanation:"Generators must be kept away from the tents because of the risk of carbon monoxide (CO) gas; fuel is kept in a dedicated, separate location."},{question:"Three soldiers in one tent wake up with headaches and dizziness; a fuel heater was burning all night. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out into fresh air and call 122 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Open the tent door and let them go back to sleep inside",correct:!1},{text:"Give them painkillers and keep the heater burning for warmth",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the heater down and stay inside the tent with them",correct:!1}],explanation:"These are signs of carbon monoxide poisoning: everyone out into fresh air, call 122 from outside and do not go back in until the place is declared safe."},{question:"Which place is the best choice for a camp?",options:[{text:"Higher ground away from valley floors and large trees, with a clear way out",correct:!0},{text:"A flat dry riverbed, because it is level and sheltered from the wind",correct:!1},{text:"Under a group of large trees, for shade and shelter from rain",correct:!1},{text:"Next to the fuel store, so that refueling is quick and easy",correct:!1}],explanation:"High ground protects from floods, staying away from large trees protects from falling branches and lightning, and a clear way out makes evacuation possible."},{question:"A thunderstorm reaches the camp. Where are the soldiers safest?",options:[{text:"Inside a solid building or a hard-topped vehicle",correct:!0},{text:"Inside their tents with the doors closed tightly",correct:!1},{text:"Under the tallest tree at the edge of the camp",correct:!1},{text:"Lying flat on open ground away from the tents",correct:!1}],explanation:"A tent gives no protection from lightning; go into a solid building or a hard-topped vehicle and wait until 30 minutes after the last thunder."}],steps:[{title:"Choose a safe site",detail:"High ground away from valley floors and dry riverbeds, not under large trees, with a clear way in and out.",critical:!0},{title:"Lay out the zones",detail:"Tents in rows with space between them; the fire area away from the tents; latrines away from water and food and downwind; the first aid point in the center; fuel in its own store."},{title:"Set up fire safety",detail:"Fire extinguishers near the tents and fire areas, a fire watch at all times and a known alarm signal.",critical:!0},{title:"Place generators outside",detail:"Outdoors only and away from the tents; never inside a tent, even with the door open.",critical:!0},{title:"Plan for emergencies",detail:"The assembly point, clear routes, the tent list for the headcount and the camp location given to 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Check every day",detail:"Walk the camp: heaters, wires, fuel, extinguishers, drainage and exit routes; fix any problem at once."}],warnings:["Never run a generator, charcoal grill or fuel heater inside a tent, even with the door open: carbon monoxide can kill sleeping people without warning.","Never pitch tents on a valley floor or in a dry riverbed: a flood can arrive quickly even when it is not raining at the camp.","Never keep fuel next to the tents or the fire area: a small fire can become a large one."],tips:["Walk the whole site before you set up and look for signs of flooding, loose rocks and dead branches.","Before you set up, ask the competent authorities whether the area has a risk of mines or unexploded ordnance.","Mark the routes to the assembly point so that they can be found at night.","If your unit has CO alarms, put them in the sleeping tents where heating is used."],activity:{prompt:"A cold night in camp. The soldiers in one tent want to bring the small generator inside to run a heater, and they say they will leave the door open. You are in charge of the tent. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"I allow it, because the open door lets in enough air",correct:!1,feedback:"No: an open door does not protect against carbon monoxide; generators run outdoors only, away from the tents."},{text:"I refuse; the generator stays outside away from the tents, and we use warm clothing, sleeping bags and the heating that command has approved",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: CO can kill people in their sleep; warmth must come from safe means."},{text:"I allow it if one soldier stays awake to watch the generator",correct:!1,feedback:"No: carbon monoxide has no color or smell; the soldier on watch can be poisoned too, without noticing."},{text:"I allow it for a short time only and then we switch it off",correct:!1,feedback:"No: CO can build up quickly in a closed tent; there is no safe time for a generator indoors."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your camp is beside a small stream on a valley floor. It is night and heavy rain is falling in the mountains upstream; the stream is rising fast. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I raise the alarm, wake everyone and move them to higher ground by the planned route",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: rising water calls for action at once; people go to higher ground first, equipment later."},{text:"I wait until morning to see whether the water keeps rising",correct:!1,feedback:"No: flood water can rise very fast at night; waiting can cut off the way out."},{text:"I drive the vehicles across the stream to the other bank before it gets deeper",correct:!1,feedback:"No: never drive into flood water; moving water can carry a vehicle away."},{text:"I put sandbags around the tents and keep everyone inside",correct:!1,feedback:"No: sandbags do not protect people from a flood; people must move to higher ground."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point on higher ground, the headcount shows that two soldiers from a tent near the stream are missing. Water is now covering the road. What now?",options:[{text:"I report it to the commander and call 115 with the location; no one goes into the flood water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: trained rescue teams do the search; people who go into the water often become victims themselves."},{text:"Two strong swimmers go into the water to look for them",correct:!1,feedback:"No: moving flood water knocks adults down; the rescuers become casualties."},{text:"I drive a truck through the water to the tent to pick them up",correct:!1,feedback:"No: water can carry a vehicle away; never drive into flood water."},{text:"I wait until the water goes down and then report them missing",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the report must go out at once so that rescue teams can start searching."}]}],realCase:"On a cold night in a field camp, a soldier woke up with a headache and felt dizzy. Two other soldiers in the same tent felt ill as well. A small generator was running beside the tent door, and the wind was carrying its exhaust inside. The squad leader suspected carbon monoxide: he got everyone out into fresh air at once and called 122 from outside. No one went back in until the tent had been opened up and checked. The next day the generator was set up further from the tents and downwind of them, and CO alarms were placed in the sleeping tents."},{id:3,code:"MIL-103",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Field first aid (MARCH)",subtitle:"Tactical first aid",summary:"First aid in field conditions: the MARCH sequence",blocks:[{body:"Field first aid differs from ordinary first aid: speed and the order of actions matter more. The MARCH sequence from Tactical Combat Casualty Care (TCCC) saves lives."},{heading:"1. The MARCH sequence",body:"Order of first aid actions:",points:["M - Massive Hemorrhage: life-threatening bleeding comes first; direct pressure, wound packing, tourniquet.","A - Airway: open the airway.","R - Respiration: check breathing and look for chest wounds.","C - Circulation: blood circulation and signs of shock.","H - Hypothermia / Head Injury: keep the casualty warm and check for head injuries."]},{heading:"2. Tourniquet",body:"For life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg:",points:["Position: 5-7 cm above the wound, not over a joint.","Tightening: turn the windlass until the bleeding stops.","Time: write the time of application on the tourniquet or on the casualty's forehead.","Never loosen it in the field; only the medical team at the hospital may remove it.","Evacuate the casualty quickly (MEDEVAC) and tell the medical team the time of application."]},{heading:"3. Recovery position",body:"For an unconscious person who is breathing normally and has no suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury:",points:["1. Roll the casualty onto their side.","2. Place their upper hand under their cheek.","3. Bend the knee of the upper leg so that they do not roll onto their stomach.","4. Tilt the head slightly back so that the airway stays open.","5. Keep checking breathing until help arrives (122 or MEDEVAC)."]},{heading:"4. Burns",body:"Treating burns in the field:",points:["Cooling: 20 minutes with clean, cool running water.","Covering: sterile gauze or clean plastic wrap, without wrapping tightly.","Do not use ice!","Do not use creams, oil or toothpaste; do not burst blisters.","Large burns, or burns to the face, hands or joints: evacuate quickly (122 or MEDEVAC)."]},{heading:"5. Safety first and calling for help",body:"Before you treat a casualty:",points:["Check the scene for danger: fire, fuel, traffic, unstable structures or objects that may be explosive; your safety comes first.","Call 122 or request MEDEVAC according to your unit's procedure; for fire or rescue, call 115 as well.","Wear gloves if you have them, and tell the casualty who you are and what you are going to do.","Do not move a casualty with a suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed.","If the casualty does not respond and is not breathing normally: start CPR (30:2, or chest compressions only if you are not trained) and call 122."]},{heading:"6. Wound packing and shock",body:"When a tourniquet cannot be used:",points:["Bleeding from the neck, armpit or groin: pack the wound tightly with gauze or a clean cloth and press hard with both hands.","Keep pressing without stopping until the medical team takes over; if blood soaks through, add more on top and do not remove the first dressing.","Signs of shock: pale, cold and sweaty skin, fast breathing, a fast pulse, weakness, anxiety or confusion.","For shock: lay the casualty down, keep them warm and off the cold ground, and keep checking the bleeding and breathing.","Give nothing to eat or drink, and evacuate quickly (122 or MEDEVAC)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_tourniquet.mp4",title:"Tourniquet",description:"How to use it"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/mil_recovery.png",title:"Recovery position",description:"For an unconscious casualty who is breathing"}],quiz:[{question:"In the MARCH sequence, what comes first?",options:[{text:"Breathing and chest wounds",correct:!1},{text:"Life-threatening bleeding",correct:!0},{text:"Circulation and signs of shock",correct:!1},{text:"Cold",correct:!1}],explanation:"M comes first, because in the field bleeding is the leading cause of preventable death."},{question:"When do we use a tourniquet?",options:[{text:"For all wounds, to stop any bleeding quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg",correct:!0},{text:"Bleeding from a large wound on the head or neck",correct:!1},{text:"Bleeding from a wound in the abdomen or chest",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only for life-threatening bleeding from the arms and legs; write down the time of application and never loosen it in the field."},{question:"Where should a tourniquet be placed?",options:[{text:"Directly on the wound, to press on the bleeding vessel",correct:!1},{text:"Over the nearest joint, where it grips most firmly",correct:!1},{text:"Below the wound, between the wound and the hand or foot",correct:!1},{text:"5-7 cm above the wound, not over a joint",correct:!0}],explanation:"The lesson places the tourniquet 5-7 cm above the wound, not over a joint; write the time of application on the tourniquet or on the casualty's forehead."},{question:"A casualty is bleeding heavily from the groin, where a tourniquet cannot be placed. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Pack the wound tightly with gauze and press hard",correct:!0},{text:"Tie a tourniquet around the waist above the wound",correct:!1},{text:"Raise both legs and wait for the bleeding to slow",correct:!1},{text:"Pour cold water on the wound to close the vessels",correct:!1}],explanation:"Bleeding from the neck, armpit or groin is packed tightly and pressed without stopping until the medical team takes over."},{question:"A casualty with a tourniquet on his leg says it hurts badly and asks you to loosen it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it tight, explain why and tell the medical team the time",correct:!0},{text:"Loosen it for a minute so that the pain eases, then retighten it",correct:!1},{text:"Remove it and press on the wound with a clean bandage instead",correct:!1},{text:"Loosen it a little every few minutes during transport",correct:!1}],explanation:"A tourniquet is painful but is never loosened in the field; only the medical team at the hospital removes it."},{question:"After falling from a vehicle, a soldier is unconscious but breathing normally and may have a back injury. The place is safe. What is right?",options:[{text:"Keep him as he is, keep checking his breathing and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Roll him onto his side into the recovery position to protect the airway",correct:!1},{text:"Sit him up against something so that he can breathe more easily",correct:!1},{text:"Carry him to a vehicle and drive him to hospital at once",correct:!1}],explanation:"With a suspected back injury the recovery position is not used; do not move him unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed, and keep checking his breathing."}],steps:[{title:"Make the scene safe",detail:"Look for danger before you approach; your safety comes first. Call 122 or request MEDEVAC.",critical:!0},{title:"M — Massive bleeding",detail:"Direct pressure, wound packing and, for an arm or leg, a tourniquet 5-7 cm above the wound, not over a joint; write down the time.",critical:!0},{title:"A — Airway",detail:"If the casualty is unconscious, open the airway. If breathing normally with no suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury: the recovery position.",critical:!0},{title:"R — Respiration",detail:"Check breathing and look for chest wounds; if breathing is not normal, start CPR and call 122; tell the medical team about any chest wound.",critical:!0},{title:"C — Circulation",detail:"Look for signs of shock and check again that the bleeding is controlled; lay the casualty down."},{title:"H — Hypothermia and head injury",detail:"Cover the casualty and protect them from the cold ground; after a head injury, watch for confusion or loss of consciousness; tell the medical team the tourniquet time."}],warnings:["Never loosen or remove a tourniquet in the field: only the medical team at the hospital may remove it.","Do not give the casualty food, drink or medicines; medicines are decided by the medical team.","Do not put a casualty with a suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury in the recovery position."],tips:["Practice putting a tourniquet on yourself and on others with a trainer until you can do it quickly.","Keep a marker pen with your tourniquet to write down the time.","Keep a blanket or an emergency cover in every vehicle kit.","Check your first aid kit before every task and replace what has been used."],activity:{prompt:"During building work on the base, a sheet of metal cuts deep into a soldier's forearm; bright red blood is spurting and he is conscious. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I apply a tourniquet above the wound, not over the elbow, write down the time and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: spurting bleeding from an arm or leg is life-threatening; an early tourniquet saves the life."},{text:"I wash the wound well with water first so that it does not get infected",correct:!1,feedback:"No: washing wastes time while he loses blood; stop the bleeding first."},{text:"I raise his arm above his head and wait for the bleeding to stop",correct:!1,feedback:"No: raising the limb is not taught for life-threatening bleeding; use pressure and a tourniquet."},{text:"I wrap a loose bandage around the arm and check it again later",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a loose bandage does not stop spurting bleeding; strong pressure or a tourniquet is needed at once."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A vehicle has rolled over on a field track. There are two casualties: one lies quietly, bleeding heavily from his thigh; the other is shouting, with a broken arm. The scene is safe. Who do you treat first?",options:[{text:"The quiet casualty who is bleeding heavily from his thigh",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: massive bleeding comes first in MARCH; a person who is shouting is breathing."},{text:"The casualty who is shouting, because his pain is the worst",correct:!1,feedback:"No: pain is not the measure; life-threatening bleeding comes first."},{text:"Neither; I wait for the medic to decide",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a person can bleed to death before help arrives; act now."},{text:"I first carry both casualties to the vehicle for evacuation",correct:!1,feedback:"No: moving them before the bleeding is controlled loses blood and time."}]},{prompt:"The tourniquet is on and the thigh bleeding has stopped. Now the casualty is pale, cold and sweaty and is becoming confused. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I lay him down, cover him against the cold, recheck the tourniquet and breathing, give nothing by mouth and speed up the evacuation",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: these are signs of shock; warmth, rechecking and fast evacuation."},{text:"I loosen the tourniquet a little so that blood can reach the leg again",correct:!1,feedback:"No: never loosen a tourniquet in the field; the bleeding can start again."},{text:"I give him water with sugar to build up his strength",correct:!1,feedback:"No: give nothing to eat or drink; he may need surgery and may vomit."},{text:"I sit him up against the vehicle so that he can breathe more easily",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a casualty in shock is laid down; sitting up can make shock worse."}]}],realCase:"A patrol vehicle rolled over on a mountain road. One soldier was bleeding heavily from his arm. A teammate applied a tourniquet above the wound, wrote the time on it and called for MEDEVAC. While they waited, the soldier became pale, cold and confused. The team laid him on a blanket, covered him, checked his breathing and the tourniquet again and gave him nothing to drink. When the medical team arrived, the teammate told them when the tourniquet had been applied and everything that had been done. Fast bleeding control and a clear handover were exactly what the team had practiced in their drills."},{id:4,code:"MIL-104",icon:"Radio",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Emergency communications",subtitle:"Communicating in an emergency",summary:"Communication systems during emergencies",blocks:[{body:"Good communication is the key to success. In disasters, communication networks may stop working."},{heading:"1. Communication systems",body:"Means of communication:",points:["Radio: the primary means, always ready.","Cell phone: if there is network coverage.","Satellite: for remote locations.","Light: visual signals.","Sound: sirens, whistles, shouting."]},{heading:"2. The correct message",body:"How to pass a message:",points:["Location: accurate coordinates.","Situation: what has happened.","Numbers: how many people and how many casualties.","Needs: what is required.","Repetition: to confirm the message."]},{heading:"3. Emergency codes",body:"Examples of color codes (codes differ from one organization to another; follow your command's instructions):",points:["Code Red: fire.","Code Blue: medical emergency.","Code Black: explosive threat.","Code Yellow: warning and standby.","Code Green: safe, the situation is over."]},{heading:"4. Calling the emergency numbers",body:"115 Civil Defense (fire, rescue, collapse, flood, gas); 122 ambulance; 104 police. Give the information in this order:",points:["Location first: area, road, a landmark and the floor; coordinates as well if you have them.","What happened and how many people are hurt or trapped.","Dangers: fire, fuel, gas, ammunition or suspicious objects.","Your name and phone number.","Do not hang up until you are told to, and answer the questions calmly."]},{heading:"5. Radio discipline",body:"Everyone shares the radio, so:",points:["Listen before you talk, and plan your message before you press the button.","Speak briefly, clearly and slowly; say numbers and place names clearly.","Ask the receiver to read back the location, numbers and needs so that you are sure they are right.","An emergency message has priority; clear the channel for it according to your unit's procedure.","Check batteries and spare power every day, and follow your unit's security rules on what may be said on open channels."]},{heading:"6. When the networks fail",body:"Plan before the emergency:",points:["Agree in advance on a backup means of communication and a meeting point.","Runner: a person who carries a written message, so that nothing is forgotten.","Visual and sound signals set out in the plan; three sounds, three fires or three lights = a call for help.","Message log: write down every message with its time.","Follow the instructions of your command and Civil Defense on how to reach 115."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_radio.mp4",title:"Radio",description:"Using the radio"}],quiz:[{question:"What does Code Red mean?",options:[{text:"Safe",correct:!1},{text:"Medical emergency",correct:!1},{text:"Fire",correct:!0},{text:"Warning",correct:!1}],explanation:"Code Red = fire."},{question:"What should a correct emergency message contain?",options:[{text:"The location, the situation, the number of casualties and the needs",correct:!0},{text:"Only your name and unit, so that the base can call you back",correct:!1},{text:"The situation and a request for help; the location can be sent later on",correct:!1},{text:"Your rank, the time and the weather conditions at the scene",correct:!1}],explanation:"A correct message gives the accurate coordinates of the location, what has happened, how many people and casualties there are and what is needed; it is repeated to confirm it."},{question:"Why must you follow your command's instructions on emergency color codes?",options:[{text:"Because the colors are set by international law for all forces",correct:!1},{text:"Because codes differ from one organization to another",correct:!0},{text:"Because only commanders are allowed to say the codes aloud",correct:!1},{text:"Because the codes change every day for security reasons",correct:!1}],explanation:"Codes differ from one organization to another, so the colors in this lesson are only examples; follow your command's instructions."},{question:"What do you say first in an emergency call?",options:[{text:"The exact location with a clear landmark",correct:!0},{text:"Your name, rank and unit",correct:!1},{text:"Your opinion on what caused it",correct:!1},{text:"The weather conditions at the scene",correct:!1}],explanation:"Location comes first, so the team can set off even if the call is cut; then what happened, casualties, dangers, name and number."},{question:"You have called 115 and want to rush off to help, but the operator is still asking questions. What is right?",options:[{text:"Stay on the line until the operator says I can hang up",correct:!0},{text:"Hang up, because I have already given them the location",correct:!1},{text:"Hand the phone to a stranger and leave",correct:!1},{text:"Hang up and call back again in an hour",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not hang up until you are told to: the operator's questions help the team on its way, and you may get first aid instructions."},{question:"After an earthquake the mobile network is down and you must get a report to command. What is the best way?",options:[{text:"The radio or the backup means in the plan, or a runner with a written message",correct:!0},{text:"Wait until the network returns, because the radio is not reliable",correct:!1},{text:"Send a runner to repeat the message from memory, unwritten",correct:!1},{text:"Post the message on social media so that everyone sees it",correct:!1}],explanation:"When networks fail, use the backup means in the plan; a written message protects the information from being forgotten."}],steps:[{title:"Gather the facts",detail:"Before you call, know the location, what happened, how many are hurt and what the dangers are."},{title:"Choose the right means",detail:"The radio for command; 115 for fire and rescue and 122 for medical casualties. If the command channel is busy, 115 and 122 do not wait.",critical:!0},{title:"Give the location first",detail:"The location, then what happened, casualties, dangers and your name and number.",critical:!0},{title:"Confirm",detail:"Ask the receiver to read the message back; correct any mistake at once."},{title:"Stay on and update",detail:"Do not hang up until you are told to; send an update when the situation changes and log every message.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never hang up an emergency call before the operator tells you to: they may still need an important detail.","Do not block an emergency channel with routine messages: a life-saving message may not get through.","Do not give a guessed location as if it were certain; say what you know and describe clear landmarks."],tips:["Learn how to describe where your post is: the road name, clear landmarks and the coordinates.","Carry spare batteries and a charger.","Practice reading back messages in drills until it becomes a habit.","Keep a card with 115, 122 and 104 in your vehicle and your kit."],activity:{prompt:"You are at a post. A truck crashes on the nearby road and catches fire; two people are trapped inside. The command radio channel is busy. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 115 and 122, give the location first, then the situation and the dangers, and stay on the line; then I inform command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: lives cannot wait for the channel to clear; 115 handles the rescue and 122 comes for the casualties."},{text:"I wait until the command channel is clear and ask for permission",correct:!1,feedback:"No: every minute matters to the people trapped; calling 115 and 122 needs no permission."},{text:"I send a text message to a friend who works in Civil Defense",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a text may be read late or not arrive at all; a direct call to 115 is the right way."},{text:"I shout to passers-by to help, without calling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"No: passers-by have neither training nor equipment; without a call, the rescue teams will not come."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a flood the mobile network at your post has stopped working. A family is stranded on the roof of a house near the post. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I report to command by radio: the location first, then the number of people, the danger and the needs, and ask them to pass it to 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: when there is no mobile network, the radio is the primary means; a complete message gets rescuers there fast."},{text:"I wait until the mobile network comes back so that I can call 115",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the network may be down for a long time; use the backup means."},{text:"I go into the water myself to bring the family out",correct:!1,feedback:"No: moving flood water sweeps people away; rescue is work for trained teams."},{text:'I send a runner without a written message to say "help is needed"',correct:!1,feedback:"No: a message without a location and numbers does not help; if you need a runner, give them a complete written message."}]},{prompt:'The commander says over the radio: "Repeat your message." The water is still rising. What do you do?',options:[{text:"I repeat it clearly: location, number of people, danger and needs, adding that the water is rising; I keep watching the place and send updates",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: repeating prevents mistakes, and updates keep the rescue team informed."},{text:'I only say "urgent help!" so as not to waste time',correct:!1,feedback:"No: without the location and numbers, the team does not know where to go or what equipment to bring."},{text:"I switch off the radio to save the battery",correct:!1,feedback:"No: command needs you; keep the link open and use the spare battery."},{text:"I leave my post to guide the rescuers without telling command",correct:!1,feedback:"No: command loses contact with you; report any move you make."}]}],realCase:"During a heavy snowstorm, power and the mobile network failed in a mountain area. The guards at a post saw that a family's car was stuck on the nearby road. They sent a complete radio message to command: the location with clear landmarks (the small bridge and the road sign), the number of people, that a child was with them and that the road was blocked by snow. Command read the message back and passed it to 115. The guards kept sending updates and wrote every message in the log. The rescue team found the place quickly because the description of the location was clear."},{id:5,code:"MIL-105",icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Protection against CBRN hazards",subtitle:"Chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear",summary:"Protection against chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear hazards",blocks:[{body:"Chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear (CBRN) hazards are a real threat to military forces and civilians. Peshmerga must recognize these hazards and know how to protect themselves."},{heading:"1. Chemical hazard (C)",body:"Chemical agents:",points:["Nerve agents (such as Sarin and VX): the most lethal.","Choking agents (such as Chlorine and Phosgene): damage the lungs.","Blister agents (such as Mustard): burn the skin, eyes and lungs.","Tear gas (CS): used in training to test the mask.","Signs: tears, runny nose, chest tightness, coughing, an unusual smell.","Action: put on your mask immediately, raise the alarm and leave the area upwind (toward the direction the wind is coming from) and to high ground."]},{heading:"2. Biological hazard (B)",body:"Biological agents:",points:["Anthrax: a bacterium; it may appear as a white powder.","Plague: a bacterium; the pneumonic form spreads from person to person through the air.","Smallpox: a virus; spreads quickly.","Signs: fever, coughing, weakness; they appear after several days or weeks.","Protection: mask, gloves, health monitoring and keeping away from the sick.","Treatment: antibiotics or vaccine, as decided by a doctor."]},{heading:"3. Radiological (R) and nuclear (N)",body:"The hazard of radioactive radiation:",points:["Dirty bomb: explosives + a radioactive material.","Nuclear: the detonation of a nuclear weapon.","Protection: Time + Distance + Shielding.","Shielding: thick walls, concrete, earth.","Decontamination: remove your outer clothing and wash your body with water and soap.","Time: the less time you spend nearby, the better."]},{heading:"4. Protective equipment (PPE)",body:"MOPP (Mission Oriented Protective Posture) levels: each level adds an item to the level before it:",points:["A gas mask with a CBRN filter, together with protective clothing, boots and gloves, must always be at hand.","MOPP 1: the protective overgarment is worn.","MOPP 2: MOPP 1 + protective overboots.","MOPP 3: MOPP 2 + the gas mask and its hood.","MOPP 4: MOPP 3 + gloves: full protection.","Atropine auto-injector: for nerve agent poisoning, according to medical instructions; and continuous training on MOPP."]},{heading:"5. Suspected chemical release: what to do",body:"Speed and direction matter:",points:["Hold your breath and put on your mask at once; warn others with the agreed signal.","Get out of the cloud upwind and to higher ground; do not walk through the cloud or into low places where gas collects.","If you cannot get out: go inside, close doors and windows, switch off the ventilation and wait for official instructions.","Once out of the area: take off contaminated outer clothing carefully, put it in a sealed bag and wash skin and hair with soap and plenty of water.","Do not eat, drink, smoke or touch your face in the area.","Report to command and 115: location, time, signs, wind direction and number of casualties."]},{heading:"6. Working with Civil Defense and specialist teams",body:"At a CBRN incident, the specialist teams lead:",points:["Civil Defense and the specialist teams set the danger area and the safe area; follow their instructions.","Do not enter the danger area without the right protective equipment and training: a rescuer who becomes contaminated is one more casualty.","Casualties and everyone who was exposed are decontaminated as directed by the specialist team.","Keep people who were exposed apart from the others and write down their names for medical follow-up.","Radiation: get inside, stay inside and stay tuned to official announcements."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_cbrn.mp4",title:"CBRN",description:"Recognition and protection"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/mil_mopp.png",title:"MOPP levels",description:"4 levels"}],quiz:[{question:"What does CBRN stand for?",options:[{text:"Command, Base, Radio, Network",correct:!1},{text:"Chemical, Biological, Radiological, Nuclear",correct:!0},{text:"Chemical, Bombs, Rockets, Nerve agents",correct:!1},{text:"Civil defense, Border, Rescue, Natural disasters",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chemical, Biological, Radiological, Nuclear."},{question:"What are the three factors of protection against radiation?",options:[{text:"Water, food, air",correct:!1},{text:"Time, distance, shielding",correct:!0},{text:"Running, pressure, heat",correct:!1},{text:"Radio, cell phone, satellite",correct:!1}],explanation:"Short time, greater distance and thick shielding (Time, Distance, Shielding)."},{question:"What is Atropine used for?",options:[{text:"Burns and skin injuries",correct:!1},{text:"Bleeding from large wounds",correct:!1},{text:"Nerve agent poisoning",correct:!0},{text:"Fractures",correct:!1}],explanation:"Atropine is a drug against nerve agent poisoning."},{question:"A toxic gas cloud is drifting toward your position. Where do you go?",options:[{text:"Upwind and to higher ground",correct:!0},{text:"Downwind, the same way as the cloud",correct:!1},{text:"Down to low ground or a basement",correct:!1},{text:"Into the cloud to warn the people there",correct:!1}],explanation:"Leave upwind (toward where the wind is coming from) and to higher ground; many gases collect in low places."},{question:"A chemical cloud is passing outside and you cannot leave the building. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay inside, close doors and windows and switch off the ventilation",correct:!0},{text:"Open all the windows so that the gas blows through the building",correct:!1},{text:"Run outside to see which way the cloud is moving",correct:!1},{text:"Turn on the fans to push the gas back outside",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you cannot get out, stay inside, close doors and windows, switch off the ventilation and follow official instructions."},{question:"A colleague wants to go into a contaminated area without a mask to pull a casualty out. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Not without protection and training; report it and let the specialist team go in",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly and hold your breath the whole time you pull him out",correct:!1},{text:"Go in, but first cover your mouth and nose with a wet cloth",correct:!1},{text:"Wait a minute for the cloud to thin out, then go in and get him",correct:!1}],explanation:"An unprotected rescuer becomes another casualty; only trained teams with protective equipment go into the contaminated area."}],steps:[{title:"Recognize and warn",detail:"Tears, runny nose, chest tightness, coughing, an unusual smell or people collapsing for no clear reason: raise the alarm at once.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect yourself",detail:"Hold your breath and put on your mask; the MOPP level as ordered.",critical:!0},{title:"Move out of the cloud",detail:"Leave upwind and to higher ground; if you cannot, shelter in a closed building.",critical:!0},{title:"Decontaminate",detail:"Take off your outer clothing and seal it in a bag; wash your skin with soap and water.",critical:!0},{title:"Report",detail:"To command and 115: location, time, signs, wind direction and number of casualties."},{title:"Follow the specialist team",detail:"Stay in the place they assign to you and do not leave without a medical check."}],warnings:["Never go into a contaminated area to rescue someone without protective equipment and training: rescuers become casualties.","Do not give any medicine or antidote unless medical instructions or the medical team direct it.","Do not eat, drink, smoke or touch your face in a contaminated area: this is how the agent gets into the body."],tips:["In training, check that your mask seals well on your face and that the filter is in date.","Practice putting on your mask quickly in drills.","Learn to read the wind direction from flags, smoke or dust.","Keep a clean change of clothes in a sealed bag in your kit."],activity:{prompt:"At a water treatment station near your post, a gas cylinder is leaking: a greenish-yellow cloud with a sharp smell is drifting toward the post, and people nearby are coughing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I put on my mask, raise the alarm, move people upwind and to higher ground and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: choking agents such as chlorine damage the lungs; get out of the cloud upwind and uphill and call 115."},{text:"I run to the cylinder and close its valve without protection",correct:!1,feedback:"No: going into the cloud without protection makes you a casualty; stopping the leak is a job for specialists."},{text:"I lead people downhill to the low ground by the river",correct:!1,feedback:"No: this kind of gas collects in low places; go upwind and to higher ground."},{text:"I cover my face with a wet cloth and stay to help the people who are coughing",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a wet cloth is not real protection, and staying in the cloud harms you too; get everyone out of it first."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"After an explosion near a storage site, several soldiers have watery eyes, chest tightness and a cough, and there is an unusual smell. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Mask on, raise the alarm and leave upwind to higher ground with the others",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: these are signs of a chemical agent; protection, the alarm and getting out of the cloud come first."},{text:"I go to the place of the explosion to find out what was stored there",correct:!1,feedback:"No: going near the source takes you into the cloud; identifying the agent is a job for the specialist teams."},{text:"I wait to see whether the symptoms pass before raising the alarm",correct:!1,feedback:"No: every second inside the cloud adds to the poisoning; the alarm is immediate."},{text:"I take shelter in the basement of the nearest building",correct:!1,feedback:"No: chemical vapors can collect in low places such as basements; go upwind and uphill."}]},{prompt:"You are now out of the area, upwind. Two soldiers have liquid on their uniforms and their eyes are still watering. What next?",options:[{text:"I help them take off their outer clothing and wash their skin with soap and plenty of water, keep them apart from the others and inform 115 and the medical team",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: removing contaminated clothing and washing quickly reduces exposure, and keeping them apart stops it spreading."},{text:"I drive them to hospital in a vehicle with the others without telling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the contamination spreads to others and to the hospital; the specialist teams and the hospital must know first."},{text:"I let them keep their uniforms on until they reach the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"No: taking off contaminated outer clothing quickly reduces the agent on the body; do it as soon as you are out."},{text:"I give them milk to drink to protect them from the poison",correct:!1,feedback:"No: no drinks or home remedies; treatment is decided by the medical team."}]}],realCase:"At a water treatment station near a checkpoint, a chlorine cylinder began to leak. The soldiers at the checkpoint saw a greenish-yellow cloud being carried by the wind toward the village. They put on their masks, sounded the siren and led people to a hill upwind. The checkpoint commander called 115 and gave the location, the wind direction and the number of people who were coughing. No one went to close the valve; the hazardous materials team stopped the leak. The people who had been exposed took off their outer clothing, were washed and were checked by the medical team."},{id:6,code:"MIL-106",icon:"Bomb",accent:"#ea580c",title:"The danger of mines and IEDs",subtitle:"Mine awareness and IED safety",summary:"Recognizing the danger of mines and improvised explosive devices (IEDs) and protecting yourself",blocks:[{body:"Mines and improvised explosive devices are a clear danger in the Kurdistan Region. This danger does not threaten soldiers alone: civilians also become victims."},{heading:"1. Types of mines",body:"Main mine types:",points:["Anti-personnel mine (AP): detonated by the pressure of a foot.","Anti-tank/anti-vehicle mine (AT): needs greater pressure.","Tripwire mine: a thin wire that is hard to see.","Claymore: detonated remotely.","Cluster munitions: small and colorful; children see them as toys!",'Signs of a minefield: red signs (a skull or the word "Mines"), red-painted stones, surrounding wire fences; never cross them.']},{heading:"2. IEDs: improvised explosive devices",body:"Types:",points:["RCIED: detonated by remote control or cell phone.","VOIED: detonated when the victim touches it or steps on it.","Timed IED: detonated by a clock (timer).","VBIED: a vehicle packed with explosives.","Signs: unfamiliar wires, an abandoned vehicle, freshly dug earth.","If you see one: do not approach! Move away and report it."]},{heading:"3. The 5Cs of IEDs",body:"5 steps:",points:["1. Confirm: confirm from a distance; do not approach and do not touch.","2. Clear: move everyone away, at least 300 m.","3. Call: report to your command and the EOD team, and call 115.","4. Cordon: seal off the area and watch out for a secondary device.","5. Control: control the area until the EOD team arrives.","Distance: at least 300 m for an IED; more for a VBIED: about 600 m for a small car and up to 2800 m for a large truck."]},{heading:"4. Civilian awareness",body:"Educating the public:",points:["Children: do not touch unknown objects!","Farmers and builders: ask the authorities before digging; a place without warning signs is not necessarily safe, because most dangerous areas are not marked.","Shepherds: do not graze livestock in mined areas.","Learn to recognize mine warning signs; respect them and never remove them.","If you see a mine or any strange object: do not go closer, warn others and keep them away, remember the place and report it to the authorities; in an emergency call 115.","Never touch or pick one up: it means death!","If you realize you may be in a mined area: stop at once, stay still and do not move your feet, call for help and wait for a trained team to arrive."]},{heading:"5. After an explosion or a mine accident",body:"The first reaction is often the most dangerous:",points:["Do not rush into the area to help: where there is one mine or explosive object, there may be others.","Stop, stay where you are and call for help: your command, 115 and 122.","Talk to the casualty from where you are: tell them to stay still and that help is coming.","Only trained and authorized teams may enter the area, reach the casualty or clear the ground.","Once the casualty is on safe ground, give first aid: MARCH, and a tourniquet for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg."]},{heading:"6. Reporting and working with specialists",body:"A good report helps the team find the danger and deal with it safely:",points:["Where: area, road, clear landmarks and coordinates if possible; never go closer to get a better look.","What you saw from where you stood, and when; do not touch it, move it or photograph it from close up.","Who is at risk: people, homes, roads or farmland nearby.","Keep people and vehicles away until the team arrives, and meet the team at a safe place to guide them.","Never try to move, cover, burn or carry the object anywhere: dealing with it is work for authorized specialists only.","Follow the instructions of your command and the competent authorities on the official way to report mines; in an emergency call 115."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_mine.mp4",title:"Mines and IEDs",description:"Recognition and self-protection"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/mil_mine_types.png",title:"Types of mines",description:"Pictures"}],quiz:[{question:"If you see an IED, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Go closer to it",correct:!1},{text:"Touch it",correct:!1},{text:"Move away and report it",correct:!0},{text:"Get close and take a photo",correct:!1}],explanation:"Move away, move people away and inform your command and the EOD team."},{question:"What is the safe distance from a vehicle-borne bomb (VBIED)?",options:[{text:"100 m, the same for every type of vehicle",correct:!1},{text:"At least 300 m for a small car, and 600 m for larger vehicles",correct:!1},{text:"At least 600 m for a small car, and more for larger vehicles",correct:!0},{text:"50 m if you are behind a wall",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to the CISA and FBI bomb threat stand-off chart: about 600 m for a small car, about 730 m for a van or SUV and 1200-2800 m for large trucks."},{question:"Why are cluster munitions dangerous for children?",options:[{text:"Because they are large and heavy",correct:!1},{text:"Small and colorful, they look like toys",correct:!0},{text:"Because they do not explode",correct:!1},{text:"Because they are made to kill only soldiers",correct:!1}],explanation:"They are small and colorful, and children pick them up as toys."},{question:"You realize that you may be standing in a mined area. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop, stay still, call for help and wait",correct:!0},{text:"Walk back carefully in my own footprints",correct:!1},{text:"Test the ground ahead with a stick and go on",correct:!1},{text:"Run quickly to the nearest road or path",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a mined area: stop, stay still, call for help and wait; never walk back in your footprints or test the ground."},{question:"A colleague has been injured in a minefield and is calling for help. What is right?",options:[{text:"Stay out, call for help, keep others away and talk to him",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly along the path he walked in on, step by step",correct:!1},{text:"Throw him a rope and drag him over the ground to safety",correct:!1},{text:"Send the two fastest soldiers in to carry him out at once",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only trained teams go into a mined area; your job is to call for help, keep people away and reassure the casualty from where you are."},{question:"Who may clear or move a suspected mine or explosive object?",options:[{text:"Only trained and authorized specialist teams",correct:!0},{text:"Any experienced soldier who is careful enough",correct:!1},{text:"The farmer who owns the land, with the village's consent",correct:!1},{text:"The person who found it, if it looks old and rusty",correct:!1}],explanation:"Clearing or moving mines and unexploded ordnance is only for authorized specialists; old objects can still explode."}],steps:[{title:"Stop at once",detail:"When you see a warning sign or a suspicious object, or realize that you may be in a mined area, stop immediately.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay still",detail:"Do not move your feet; do not walk back in your own footsteps, test the ground or touch anything.",critical:!0},{title:"Call for help",detail:"Call your command and 115; say where you are and what you saw.",critical:!0},{title:"Warn others",detail:"Shout to the others to stop and stay where they are, and keep everyone else away.",critical:!0},{title:"Wait for the trained team",detail:"Wait for the authorized team however long it takes, and follow their instructions exactly.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never try to walk back in your footsteps, test the ground or mark a way out: wait for the trained team.","Never touch, move, collect or keep any mine or explosive object, even if it is old or rusty.","Never rush into a mined area to help a casualty: rescuers often become the next victims."],tips:["Before field work, ask your command and the mine action authorities about known danger areas.","Stay on known roads and paths that people use; no sign does not mean safe.","Teach your family: do not touch, keep away, tell an adult and call 115.","Carry a charged phone or a radio when you work in the field."],activity:{prompt:"On a patrol near an old front line, a soldier sees a red sign with a skull behind him: he has already walked past it into the field. What should he do?",options:[{text:"Stop, stay still, call for help and wait for the trained team",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in a mined area, stopping and staying still until the team arrives is the safe way."},{text:"Walk back slowly, stepping exactly in his own footprints",correct:!1,feedback:"No: walking back in your footprints is not a safe way out of a mined area; stay still and wait for the team."},{text:"Test the ground in front of him with a knife and walk out",correct:!1,feedback:"No: testing the ground is work for trained specialists only; stay still."},{text:"Run out of the field quickly before anything happens",correct:!1,feedback:"No: running means more steps on unknown ground; stop and stay still."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"An explosion is heard in a field near your post. A young shepherd lies injured in the field, and his father is running toward him. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I shout to the father to stop, keep everyone out of the field, call command, 115 and 122 and tell the boy to stay still",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: there may be more mines in the field; the aim is that no one else is hurt and that the trained team arrives fast."},{text:"I run into the field with the father to carry the boy out",correct:!1,feedback:"No: untrained rescuers often become the next victims in mined areas."},{text:"I drive a vehicle into the field to reach the boy faster",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a vehicle can also set off an explosion in a mined area; no one goes in without the trained team."},{text:"I walk in along the path the boy took, because it must be safe",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a path someone has walked is no proof of safety; there may be more mines close by."}]},{prompt:"The trained team has brought the boy out to safe ground. He is bleeding heavily from his leg. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I apply a tourniquet above the wound, write down the time, keep him warm and hand him over to 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: massive bleeding comes first in MARCH; the tourniquet time matters to the medical team."},{text:"I wash the wound first to see how deep it is",correct:!1,feedback:"No: washing wastes time while he loses blood; stop the bleeding first."},{text:"I go back into the field to fetch his lost shoe and stick",correct:!1,feedback:"No: never go into the field; whatever is left there stays there."},{text:"I give him water and food to keep up his strength",correct:!1,feedback:"No: give nothing by mouth; he may need surgery."}]}],realCase:"A farmer was plowing a field that had never had any warning signs when he saw that his plow had turned up a rusty metal object. He switched off the engine, stayed in his seat and called 115 on his phone, saying where the field was and what he had seen. He shouted to his sons, who were near the house, to stay away from the field. The police and Civil Defense kept people back until the authorized clearance team arrived. The team guided the farmer out and dealt with the object. Afterwards specialists checked the whole field before anyone worked there again."},{id:7,code:"MIL-107",icon:"Compass",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Survival and navigation",subtitle:"Survival skills and navigation",summary:"Field survival skills, navigation and rescue",blocks:[{body:"In field conditions, a Peshmerga may become separated from their team. Water, fire, shelter and direction are the four pillars of survival."},{heading:"1. Water: the first priority",body:"In general, a person survives about 3 days without water, and less in the heat:",points:["Sources: rivers, springs, rain, dew.","Boiling: 1 minute at a rolling boil (above 2000 m altitude: 3 minutes).","Purification tablets: chlorine or iodine, following the instructions on the packet.","Filtering: filter through a clean cloth, then boil.","Do not drink stagnant water without purifying it.","Always carry a bottle of water with you."]},{heading:"2. Fire",body:"Fire = warmth + light + signaling:",points:["The fire triangle: fuel + oxygen + heat.","Dry wood: start with small twigs.","Lighting: a lighter, matches, or flint and steel.","Leave gaps between the pieces of wood so that air can reach the fire.","Choose a spot cleared of dry grass and ring the fire with stones.","Three fires in a triangle = a distress signal (SOS)."]},{heading:"3. Shelter",body:"Protection from the weather:",points:["Caves: the best shelters, but beware of rockfalls and animals.","Tree branches and boughs.","Rocks and earth.","The shelter entrance must not face the wind.","A fire near the shelter, but at a safe distance.","Protect yourself from the cold of the ground (put branches, grass or cardboard underneath you)."]},{heading:"4. Navigation",body:"Without GPS:",points:["Compass: direction and azimuth.","The sun: rises in the east and sets in the west.","The North Star (Polaris): shows the direction of north.","Moss on trees is not a reliable sign for finding north.","Water flows downhill; follow a river toward villages and roads.","Three sounds, three fires or three lights = a distress signal (SOS)."]},{heading:"5. If you are separated from your team",body:"Think before you move:",points:["Stop, stay calm, think and look around; panic leads to bad decisions.","If your team knows your route, it is usually safer to stay where you are: the search starts where you were last seen.","Try to make contact: radio, phone or the agreed signals.","Make yourself easy to see: a brightly colored cloth, a mirror flash or a signal fire in a cleared, safe place.","Where there may be mines, stay on known paths that people use; never cross unknown fields or old front lines.","Save your water and energy: rest in the shade in the hottest hours and move in the cooler hours."]},{heading:"6. Heat and cold",body:"The weather can kill faster than hunger:",points:["Heat: cover your head, rest in the shade, drink water regularly and avoid hard work in the hottest hours.","Heat stroke: confusion, strange behavior, a seizure or collapse in the heat; call for help and cool the person at once with water, wet cloths and fanning; give nothing to drink if they are confused.","Cold: stay dry, change wet clothes and protect yourself from the wind and the cold ground.","Hypothermia: shivering, slurred speech, clumsy movements and confusion; get the person out of the wind and wet, remove wet clothes and warm them gradually.","Watch your teammates: a person with heat or cold illness often does not notice it."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_survival.mp4",title:"Survival",description:"Core skills"}],quiz:[{question:"In general, how many days can a person survive without water?",options:[{text:"1",correct:!1},{text:"3",correct:!0},{text:"7",correct:!1},{text:"14",correct:!1}],explanation:"About 3 days (the rule of threes); in the heat the time is much shorter."},{question:"What do three fires in a triangle mean?",options:[{text:"A warning to keep away",correct:!1},{text:"SOS: a request for help",correct:!0},{text:"A forest fire is spreading",correct:!1},{text:"They have no meaning",correct:!1}],explanation:"It is an international signal requesting help (SOS)."},{question:"How long must water be boiled to make it safe to drink?",options:[{text:"Only until the first small bubbles appear at the bottom of the pot",correct:!1},{text:"10 seconds, then it must be left to cool covered",correct:!1},{text:"1 minute at a rolling boil; above 2000 m, 3 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"At least 30 minutes, or the germs will survive",correct:!1}],explanation:"Boil water for 1 minute at a rolling boil (above 2000 m altitude: 3 minutes); do not drink stagnant water without purifying it."},{question:"You are separated from your team, but they know your planned route. What is usually best?",options:[{text:"Stay near the route where they will search, and signal",correct:!0},{text:"Walk cross-country in a straight line toward the base",correct:!1},{text:"Keep moving all night so that I stay warm",correct:!1},{text:"Hide in a cave far from the route until morning",correct:!1}],explanation:"The search follows your route and starts from your last known position; staying close and signaling gets you found faster."},{question:"After hours in the heat, a teammate becomes confused and acts strangely. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call for help and cool him at once with water and fanning",correct:!0},{text:"Give him plenty of water to drink while he is confused",correct:!1},{text:"Let him sleep in the sun until he feels better",correct:!1},{text:"Tell him to keep marching to reach shade sooner",correct:!1}],explanation:"Confusion or strange behavior in the heat can be heat stroke: call for help and cool him at once; give nothing to drink while he is confused."},{question:"When you cross an area where there may be mines, which way is safest?",options:[{text:"Only on known roads and paths that people use",correct:!0},{text:"Straight across the field, because it is quicker",correct:!1},{text:"Along the edge of the field, beside the fence",correct:!1},{text:"Where the grass is short and the ground looks clear",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stay on known roads and paths that people use; ground can look clear and still hold mines."}],steps:[{title:"Stop and think",detail:"Stay calm, look around and check your water, kit and injuries before you decide anything."},{title:"Make contact",detail:"Radio, phone or the agreed signals; three signals mean a call for help.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect yourself from heat and cold",detail:"Shade or shelter, dry clothes and something between you and the ground.",critical:!0},{title:"Safe water",detail:"Drink only treated water: boiling, tablets, or filtering and then boiling.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay put or move safely",detail:"If the team knows your route, stay near the last known point. If you must move, use known paths away from possible mine areas and tell your team by radio which way you are going.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never cross unknown fields or old front lines where there may be mines: stay on known paths that people use.","Do not drink stagnant or untreated water: diarrhea weakens you quickly.","Do not light a fire inside a closed shelter or deep in a cave: smoke and carbon monoxide build up."],tips:["Before every task, tell someone your route and when you expect to be back.","Carry water, a map, a compass, a whistle, a light and a small first aid kit.","Agree on the distress signals and a meeting point with your team before you set out.","Check the weather forecast before field tasks."],activity:{prompt:"At dusk you become separated from your patrol in hills you do not know. Your radio does not work, but your team knows your planned route. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay near the last known point on the route, make a shelter, signal in threes and wait",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the team will search along your route; staying at a known point makes you easier to find."},{text:"I walk through the night across unknown fields to find a road",correct:!1,feedback:"No: at night you can fall, get lost or walk into a mined area; stay where the search will be."},{text:"I drink from a stagnant pool nearby to save the water in my bottle",correct:!1,feedback:"No: stagnant water causes illness; treat it or drink your own water."},{text:"I light a fire inside a small closed cave to keep warm",correct:!1,feedback:"No: smoke and CO build up in a closed space; light the fire at a safe distance from the shelter."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"After losing your team, you are alone on a hot afternoon. You have one bottle of water and there is no shade nearby. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I find or make shade, rest in the hottest hours, drink water as I need it and move only in the cooler hours",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: shade and rest save the body's water; moving in the heat can lead to heat stroke."},{text:"I walk fast in the sun to reach help sooner",correct:!1,feedback:"No: walking hard in the heat quickly leads to heat illness."},{text:"I take off my shirt and hat to cool down in the sun",correct:!1,feedback:"No: clothes and a hat protect you from the sun; bare skin burns and loses water faster."},{text:"I save all my water for tomorrow and drink nothing today",correct:!1,feedback:"No: not drinking when you need water leads to dehydration and heat illness."}]},{prompt:"In the morning you hear a vehicle on a ridge and see your team's vehicle in the distance. What now?",options:[{text:"I signal clearly: wave a bright cloth, use a mirror or whistle in threes and stay where they can see me",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a clear signal and staying in sight get you found quickly."},{text:"I run toward them across the slope I do not know",correct:!1,feedback:"No: unknown ground may hold hazards or mines; let them come to you."},{text:"I hide in the shelter until they come closer",correct:!1,feedback:"No: they may pass without seeing you; make yourself visible."},{text:"I light a large fire in the dry grass to make smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a fire in dry grass can get out of control; use a small, controlled fire on cleared ground."}]}],realCase:"A soldier became separated from his team in thick fog in the mountains. He remembered his training: he stopped, checked his kit and stayed on the path near the place where he had last seen his team. He built a small shelter from branches, put grass under himself against the cold ground and drank only the water in his bottle. When the fog lifted, he blew his whistle three times and waved a bright cloth. The search team, which had started from his last known position, spotted him quickly. He was cold and tired but unhurt."},{id:8,code:"MIL-108",icon:"Truck",accent:"#4b5563",title:"Vehicle and convoy safety",subtitle:"Vehicle and convoy safety",summary:"Safety in military transport and convoys",blocks:[{body:"Traffic accidents, ambushes and improvised explosive devices (IEDs) on the roads are the main hazards."},{heading:"1. Military driving rules",body:"Principles:",points:["Always wear a helmet.","Do not overload open vehicles with passengers.","Seat belts for all occupants.","Radio: continuous communication.","In dangerous areas, use only cleared routes and do not leave the road.","At night: vehicle lights according to command instructions."]},{heading:"2. Convoy organization",body:"Rules:",points:["Spacing between vehicles: 50-100 m, as ordered by the convoy commander.","One radio in every vehicle.","Medical vehicle: at the rear.","Recovery (repair) vehicle: in the middle.","Lead vehicle: 500 m ahead.","Briefing before departure."]},{heading:"3. Ambush",body:"If the convoy is ambushed:",points:["Drive quickly out of the area: do not stop!","Send a SALUTE report quickly.","Identify the direction of the attack.","MARCH for casualties once the location is secure.","Request backup.","Watch out for a secondary IED on the road."]},{heading:"4. Traffic accidents",body:"What to do:",points:["Stop in a safe place.","Place the warning triangle.","MARCH for casualties.","Call 115 and 122.","Fire: move everyone away and stay upwind; if a vehicle carrying explosives is involved in the fire (ERG Guide 112, Divisions 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5): isolate and evacuate 1600 m (1 mile) in all directions, emergency responders included; if the fire reaches the cargo, do not fight it.","Take photos and write a report."]},{heading:"5. Before departure",body:"Checks that prevent accidents:",points:["Vehicle: tires, brakes, lights, fuel and steering checked; loads tied down so that they cannot shift.","Every vehicle carries the fire safety equipment set by the unit, a first aid kit with a tourniquet and a warning triangle.","The driver is rested: fatigue slows reactions and can put the driver to sleep; change drivers on long journeys.","No phone use while driving; another person handles the radio.","Speed as ordered by the convoy commander and suited to the road and the weather.","Everyone knows the route, the stops and what to do if there is an accident."]},{heading:"6. At a road accident: working with Civil Defense",body:"Make the scene safe before you help:",points:["Park so that your vehicle protects the scene, switch on the hazard lights and place the warning triangle to warn traffic.","If it is safe, switch off the engines of the damaged vehicles and do not smoke; watch for leaking fuel.","Keep people out of the traffic lane and away from the damaged vehicles.","Do not pull out a trapped person unless there is fire or another immediate danger: Civil Defense (115) frees trapped people with the right tools.","Give 115 and 122 the location, the number of vehicles and casualties and any fuel or cargo danger, and keep the road clear for emergency vehicles."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_convoy.mp4",title:"Convoy",description:"Organization"}],quiz:[{question:"According to this lesson, what is the normal spacing between convoy vehicles?",options:[{text:"10-20 m",correct:!1},{text:"50-100 m",correct:!0},{text:"2 m",correct:!1},{text:"300-500 m",correct:!1}],explanation:"50-100 m, depending on the situation and the convoy commander's orders."},{question:"During an ambush, what will the driver do?",options:[{text:"Stop and wait for help to arrive",correct:!1},{text:"Drive quickly out of the area",correct:!0},{text:"Get out of the vehicle",correct:!1},{text:"Stop to take photos",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drive quickly out of the ambush area, if the road is open."},{question:"In dangerous areas, where should military vehicles drive?",options:[{text:"Off the road, to avoid IEDs buried in the asphalt",correct:!1},{text:"On whichever route is shortest, to spend less time exposed",correct:!1},{text:"On the shoulder beside the road, so others can pass",correct:!1},{text:"Only on cleared routes, without leaving the road",correct:!0}],explanation:"In dangerous areas, use only cleared routes and do not leave the road; IEDs on the roads are among the main hazards."},{question:"You are the first to reach a road accident on a busy road. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Stop safely, switch on the hazard lights and warn traffic",correct:!0},{text:"Run straight across the road to the damaged cars",correct:!1},{text:"Start taking photos before anyone else arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Move the damaged cars off the road before calling",correct:!1}],explanation:"First make the scene safe and warn traffic so that there is no second collision; then call 115 and 122."},{question:"Why is driving when very tired dangerous?",options:[{text:"Fatigue slows reactions and can put the driver to sleep",correct:!0},{text:"Tired drivers always drive too slowly for the convoy",correct:!1},{text:"Fatigue matters only at night, not in the daytime",correct:!1},{text:"Tired drivers use too much fuel on long journeys",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fatigue slows reactions and can put the driver to sleep, by night or by day; rest and change drivers."},{question:"A driver is trapped in a crashed car with neck pain, and there is no fire. Who frees him?",options:[{text:"The Civil Defense (115) rescue team with the right tools",correct:!0},{text:"The convoy soldiers, by pulling him through the window",correct:!1},{text:"Passers-by, as fast as they can, before help comes",correct:!1},{text:"No one; he must climb out alone when he can",correct:!1}],explanation:"With no fire or immediate danger, the trapped person stays still and the 115 team frees him with the right tools so that the neck injury does not get worse."}],steps:[{title:"Stop safely and warn traffic",detail:"Hazard lights and the warning triangle; place your vehicle so that it protects the scene.",critical:!0},{title:"Check for danger",detail:"Traffic, fire, leaking fuel and dangerous cargo.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 and 122",detail:"Location first, then the number of vehicles, casualties, trapped people and dangers.",critical:!0},{title:"MARCH for casualties",detail:"Massive bleeding first; do not move a casualty with a suspected neck or back injury unless the place is unsafe.",critical:!0},{title:"Leave trapped people to Civil Defense",detail:"Talk to them, keep them still and wait for the 115 rescue team."},{title:"Report",detail:"Once the casualties are cared for, take photos and write the report."}],warnings:["Never pull a trapped person out of a vehicle unless there is fire or another immediate danger: a spinal injury can be made worse.","Never stand in the traffic lane or between vehicles at an accident scene: a second collision is a real danger.","If fire reaches a vehicle's explosive cargo, do not fight it: move everyone away to the distance given in the traffic accidents section."],tips:["Everyone wears a seat belt on every trip, in every seat.","Rest before a long drive and change drivers when you are tired.","Check the vehicle kit before you leave: fire safety equipment, first aid kit and triangle.","Learn the convoy emergency plan and the radio call signs before you leave."],activity:{prompt:"A convoy vehicle hits a civilian car. The civilian driver is trapped, conscious and has neck pain; there is no fire and no fuel leak. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I secure the scene, call 115 and 122, keep him still, talk to him and wait for Civil Defense to free him",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: with no fire or immediate danger, moving him can make a neck injury worse; 115 frees him with the right tools."},{text:"I pull him out quickly so that he can lie down on the road",correct:!1,feedback:"No: pulling him out can make a neck injury worse, and the road is not a safe place for him."},{text:"I give him water and help him sit up straight",correct:!1,feedback:"No: sitting him up can make a neck injury worse; keep him still."},{text:"I drive on with the convoy and report the accident at the base",correct:!1,feedback:"No: you must help, secure the scene and call 115 and 122; leaving casualties is never right."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At night on a mountain road, a truck in your convoy overturns. The vehicles behind it are still moving. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I stop the convoy safely, switch on the hazard lights, place the triangles and call 115 and 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: first prevent a second collision and call for help, then go to the casualties."},{text:"Everyone runs to the overturned truck at once",correct:!1,feedback:"No: people on a dark road can be hit by traffic; make the scene safe first."},{text:"I drive on and leave the truck crew to call for help themselves",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the crew may be injured and unable to call; stop safely and help."},{text:"I push the truck back onto its wheels with another vehicle",correct:!1,feedback:"No: this can crush people and spill fuel; wait for Civil Defense."}]},{prompt:"Smoke is coming from the engine of the overturned truck. The driver has crawled out and is bleeding heavily from his arm. What now?",options:[{text:"I move him away from the truck, upwind, apply a tourniquet, write down the time, keep others back and tell 115 about the smoke",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: smoke means a fire risk, so move him away first; then control the bleeding."},{text:"I treat him right next to the truck so that I do not move him",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a fire may start; when the place is unsafe, moving the casualty comes first."},{text:"I open the engine cover to see where the smoke is coming from",correct:!1,feedback:"No: going close to a smoking engine puts you at risk; keep away and tell 115."},{text:"I pour the water from our bottles onto the engine",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a little water will not stop an engine or fuel fire, and it keeps you close to the danger."}]}],realCase:"On a long night drive, the driver of the third vehicle in a convoy fell asleep, and the vehicle left the road and ended up in a ditch. Everyone was wearing a seat belt and no one was badly hurt. The convoy commander stopped the convoy safely, the crews switched on hazard lights and placed warning triangles, and the unit medic checked the occupants. One soldier had neck pain, so he was kept still in his seat until the 115 and 122 teams arrived and took him out on a board. After that night, the unit set rest stops on long journeys and changed drivers."},{id:9,code:"MIL-109",icon:"Droplets",accent:"#0284c7",title:"Water purification and personal hygiene",subtitle:"Water purification and hygiene",summary:"Water purification and personal hygiene in the field",blocks:[{body:"Diseases from contaminated water can be more dangerous than the enemy! Contaminated water weakens forces."},{heading:"1. Water purification",body:"4 methods:",points:["Boiling: 1 minute at a rolling boil; the most reliable way to kill germs.","Chlorine/iodine tablets: follow the instructions on the packet, and wait 30 minutes.","Special filters (such as LifeStraw).","SODIS: clear plastic bottles, 6 hours in direct sunlight (if it is cloudy: 2 days).","Do not drink stagnant water without purifying it.","Order: first let the sediment settle, then filter, then disinfect."]},{heading:"2. Personal hygiene",body:"In the field:",points:["Handwashing: before eating and after using the latrine.","Clean your body regularly; if water is scarce, use a damp cloth.","Change your socks daily and keep your clothes clean.","Keep your feet clean and dry.","Minor wounds: wash them and cover them with a clean dressing.","Brush your teeth twice a day."]},{heading:"3. Food",body:"Food safety:",points:["Check the expiry date.","Cook food thoroughly (75°C at the center).","Do not leave cooked food at room temperature for more than 2 hours (above 32°C: 1 hour).","Cover food to protect it from flies and dust.","Burn or bury food waste away from the camp.","Drink plenty of clean water."]},{heading:"4. Field illnesses",body:"Common illnesses:",points:["Diarrhea: drink a water, salt and sugar solution (ORS).","Malaria: mosquito nets and insect repellent spray.","Heat stroke: move the person into the shade, cool them rapidly and call 122.","Hypothermia: rewarm gradually and remove wet clothing.","Infection: change the dressing daily, or whenever it becomes wet or dirty.","Report any illness to the medic."]},{heading:"5. Latrines, waste and handwashing",body:"Many field illnesses spread through dirty hands, flies and waste:",points:["Latrines: away from water sources and the kitchen, and downwind.","A handwashing point with soap and water next to every latrine and the kitchen.","Keep latrines covered and clean so that flies cannot carry germs to the food.","Collect rubbish in covered containers and take it away or bury it away from the camp.","Drain standing water or fill it with earth so that mosquitoes cannot breed in it."]},{heading:"6. Stopping illness from spreading in the unit",body:"One sick person can make many others ill:",points:["A person with diarrhea or vomiting does not prepare or serve food, and reports to the medic.","Do not share bottles, cups or cutlery.","Keep treated water in clean, covered containers with a tap; do not dip cups into the container.","Clean and disinfect water containers regularly.","Report early: if several people fall ill after the same food or water, tell the medic at once."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_water.mp4",title:"Water",description:"Purification"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the most reliable method of purifying water?",options:[{text:"Tablets",correct:!1},{text:"Boiling",correct:!0},{text:"A filter",correct:!1},{text:"Sunlight",correct:!1}],explanation:"Boiling for 1 minute at a rolling boil is the most reliable way to kill disease-causing germs."},{question:"For how many hours can cooked food stay at room temperature?",options:[{text:"No more than 12 hours (in hot weather: 6 hours)",correct:!1},{text:"No more than 2 hours (above 32°C: 1 hour)",correct:!0},{text:"Up to 24 hours if it is covered",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to WHO guidance, do not leave cooked food at room temperature for more than 2 hours; above 32°C, 1 hour (USDA)."},{question:"In which order should water be treated in the field?",options:[{text:"Let the sediment settle, then filter, then disinfect",correct:!0},{text:"Disinfect first, then filter, then let the sediment settle",correct:!1},{text:"Filter first, then disinfect, then let the sediment settle",correct:!1},{text:"Disinfect first, then let the sediment settle, then filter",correct:!1}],explanation:"The lesson's order is: first let the sediment settle, then filter, then disinfect."},{question:"A cook in your unit has had diarrhea since this morning. What is right?",options:[{text:"He stops handling food and reports to the medic",correct:!0},{text:"He keeps cooking but wears gloves the whole time",correct:!1},{text:"He keeps cooking if he washes his hands more often",correct:!1},{text:"He only serves the food and does not cook it himself",correct:!1}],explanation:"A person with diarrhea or vomiting does not prepare or serve food; gloves and washing are not enough to stop the spread."},{question:"Water from a clear mountain stream: what must you do before you drink it?",options:[{text:"Treat it: settle, filter and disinfect it, for example by boiling",correct:!0},{text:"Nothing, as long as the water is cold, clear and running fast",correct:!1},{text:"Just strain it through a clean cloth and drink it right away",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it in a closed bottle for an hour, then drink it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Even clear water can carry germs; the order in this lesson is settle, filter, then disinfect."},{question:"Where should a handwashing point with soap and water be?",options:[{text:"Next to the latrines and the kitchen",correct:!0},{text:"Only at the first aid point in the center",correct:!1},{text:"At the camp gate, for visitors to use",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the sleeping tents, for use at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hands are washed after the latrine and before eating and cooking, so that is where the handwashing point must be."}],steps:[{title:"Choose a safe water source",detail:"Upstream of the camp and the latrines, away from animals and waste."},{title:"Treat the water",detail:"Let the sediment settle, filter it, then disinfect it: boil for 1 minute at a rolling boil, or use tablets as the packet says.",critical:!0},{title:"Store it safely",detail:"Clean, covered containers with a tap; keep treated and untreated water apart and clearly labeled."},{title:"Wash your hands",detail:"With soap and water, before eating and cooking and after using the latrine.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect the food",detail:"Cook it thoroughly, cover it and do not leave cooked food at room temperature longer than this lesson allows."},{title:"Report illness early",detail:"Diarrhea, vomiting or fever: tell the medic; a sick person does not handle food.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never drink untreated water from rivers, springs or pools, even if it looks clear: germs cannot be seen.","A person with diarrhea or vomiting must not prepare or serve food: the illness can spread to the whole unit.","Never place latrines or rubbish near the water source or the kitchen."],tips:["Carry your own bottle and a way to treat water (tablets or a filter).","Label the containers of treated and untreated water clearly.","Check your feet every day and keep them clean and dry.","Drink clean water regularly when you work in the heat."],activity:{prompt:"The camp water truck is a day late. There is a small stream of clear water near the camp. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I let the sediment settle, filter it, boil it for 1 minute at a rolling boil and store it covered",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is the order in the lesson, and boiling is the most reliable way to kill germs."},{text:"I drink it straight away, because clear water is clean",correct:!1,feedback:"No: germs cannot be seen; clear water can make you ill too."},{text:"I use twice the number of tablets the packet says, to make it quicker",correct:!1,feedback:"No: follow the instructions on the packet; more is neither safer nor quicker."},{text:"I use it untreated only for washing fruit and brushing teeth",correct:!1,feedback:"No: water that reaches the mouth, including for washing food and brushing teeth, must be treated."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Several soldiers in one platoon have diarrhea and vomiting a few hours after the same meal. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I report to the medic at once, keep the sick soldiers away from food work, give them ORS and keep the leftover food for checking",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an early report, keeping the sick away from food and ORS stop the spread and help find the cause."},{text:"I wait a day to see whether it passes on its own",correct:!1,feedback:"No: several people ill at the same time is a sign of an outbreak; report at once."},{text:"I let the sick soldiers carry on cooking if they feel able",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a sick person can spread the illness to the whole unit through the food."},{text:"I throw away all the leftover food and say nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the medic needs to know, and the leftovers may help find the cause."}]},{prompt:"The medic suspects the water container in the kitchen. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"I empty, clean and disinfect the container, treat fresh water, check the source and remind everyone about handwashing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this removes the suspected cause and prevents new cases."},{text:"I keep using the container, but from now on boil only the water for tea",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a contaminated container spoils all the water in it; it must be cleaned."},{text:"I add a little bleach to the drinking water without measuring it",correct:!1,feedback:"No: chemicals are used only as the packet or the medic directs."},{text:"I move the kitchen next to the latrines so that they share one water point",correct:!1,feedback:"No: latrines must be away from food and water."}]}],realCase:"In a field camp, many soldiers fell ill with diarrhea over two days. The medic found that the water was kept in an open drum and that everyone dipped their own cup into it. There was no soap at the latrines either. The unit emptied and disinfected the drum, replaced it with a covered container with a tap, set up handwashing points with soap next to the latrines and the kitchen and asked the sick soldiers to stay away from cooking. The sick soldiers drank ORS and were checked by the medic. A few days later, no new cases were being reported."},{id:10,code:"MIL-110",icon:"Thermometer",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Heat and cold: weather hazards",subtitle:"Heat and cold weather operations",summary:"Heat and cold hazards and how to protect yourself",blocks:[{body:"In the Kurdistan Region, summer temperatures reach about 50°C, and in winter the mountains can drop below -15°C."},{heading:"1. Heat hazards",body:"Heat stroke kills:",points:["Heat exhaustion: headache, nausea, vomiting, heavy sweating.","Heat stroke: body temperature of 40°C or higher and altered mental state: an emergency! Cool immediately and call 122.","Water: about 1 liter per hour during heavy work; no more than 1.4 liters per hour.","Work and rest cycles according to the heat index (WBGT); rest in the shade.","Heat stroke cooling: at once, before transport; best is the whole body up to the neck in cold water for 15 minutes or until the casualty thinks clearly again, whichever comes first; if not possible: cold shower, cold wet towels or sheets, ice packs and fanning; nothing to drink while confused.","Light-colored, lightweight clothing."]},{heading:"2. Cold hazards",body:"Hypothermia and frostbite:",points:["Hypothermia: body temperature below 35°C.","Frostbite: freezing of the fingers, ears and nose.","Layered clothing.","Rewarm gradually, not with direct fire.","Frostbite: do not rub! Place it in warm water (37-39°C), only if it will not freeze again.","If shivering stops and the person becomes confused, it is an emergency! Call 122."]},{heading:"3. Layered clothing",body:"The three-layer system:",points:["1. Layer next to the skin (base layer): wicks sweat away from the body.","2. Insulating layer (insulation): such as fleece.","3. Outer layer (shell): against wind and rain.","Thick gloves.","Hat and scarf: heat is lost from any uncovered part of the body, not only the head; cover the whole body.","Waterproof boots."]},{heading:"4. Heat illness: recognize and act",body:"In the heat, watch yourself and your colleagues and act at the first sign:",points:["Heat exhaustion: move the person to the shade, loosen their clothing, cool the skin with water and give sips of water if they are fully alert.","If they do not improve quickly, vomit or get worse, treat it as an emergency and call 122.","Heat stroke: confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, a seizure or collapse in the heat; the skin may still be sweating.","Heat stroke cannot wait: call 122 and start cooling at once, as in section 1.","New arrivals are at higher risk: increase work in the heat gradually over the first days.","Buddy system: the two partners check each other for early signs."]},{heading:"5. Hypothermia: recognize and act",body:"Cold, wind and wet clothing take heat from the body quickly, even when the air is not freezing:",points:["Early signs: strong shivering, cold pale skin, clumsy hands and tiredness.","Danger signs: confusion, slurred speech, drowsiness and shivering that stops.","Move the person out of the wind and wet into a sheltered place.","Remove wet clothing gently and put on dry layers; cover the head and neck too.","Insulate the body from the cold ground: put a blanket, mat or dry branches underneath.","Warm sweet drinks only if the person is fully alert and can swallow; never alcohol.","Handle a person with severe hypothermia slowly and gently and call 122."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_weather.mp4",title:"Weather",description:"Heat and cold"}],quiz:[{question:"During heavy work in the heat, about how much water do you drink per hour?",options:[{text:"Only when you are thirsty",correct:!1},{text:"About 1 liter (no more than 1.4 liters)",correct:!0},{text:"About 2 liters (no less than 1.5 liters)",correct:!1},{text:"3 liters",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to US Army guidance (TB MED 507): about 1 liter per hour during heavy work; more than 1.4 liters per hour can lower the body's salt level."},{question:"With frostbite, which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Rubbing the frozen area or putting snow on it",correct:!0},{text:"Placing the area in warm water (37-39°C)",correct:!1},{text:"Removing wet and tight clothing",correct:!1},{text:"Taking the casualty to a warm, sheltered place",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rubbing, snow and direct fire damage the skin; rewarm with warm water (37-39°C)."},{question:"How should a heat stroke casualty be cooled, according to this lesson?",options:[{text:"Only after transport to hospital, so that cooling does not delay the trip",correct:!1},{text:"At once, before transport, ideally in cold water up to the neck",correct:!0},{text:"By giving plenty of cold water to drink while the casualty is still confused",correct:!1},{text:"Slowly, with a damp cloth on the forehead, so that the body is not shocked",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool heat stroke at once and before transport: best is the whole body up to the neck in cold water for 15 minutes or until the casualty thinks clearly again, whichever comes first; give nothing to drink while confused and call 122."},{question:"In the heat, a colleague is confused but his skin is still sweating. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Treat it as heat stroke: call 122 and cool him at once",correct:!0},{text:"Treat it as tiredness: let him rest and drink plenty of water",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until his skin is dry and hot, then treat him",correct:!1},{text:"Take his temperature first, then decide whether to cool him",correct:!1}],explanation:"Confusion in the heat is heat stroke even if the skin sweats. Call 122 and cool at once; give nothing to drink while the person is confused."},{question:"A soldier with hypothermia has become drowsy and confused. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Giving him a hot drink to wake him up",correct:!0},{text:"Removing his wet clothing and covering him with dry layers",correct:!1},{text:"Insulating him from the cold ground with a mat or blanket",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 122 and moving him gently",correct:!1}],explanation:"A drowsy or confused person gets nothing by mouth, because he may choke. Dry clothing, insulation from the ground, gentle handling and 122 are all right."},{question:"Which plan best protects a unit from heat illness?",options:[{text:"Buddy checks, shade, regular drinking and heavy work in cooler hours",correct:!0},{text:"Drinking only when thirsty, so no time is lost during heavy work",correct:!1},{text:"Dark, thick clothing against the sun and long shifts without breaks",correct:!1},{text:"The heaviest work at midday, so that the evening stays free for rest",correct:!1}],explanation:"Buddy checks, rest in the shade, regular drinking and planning heavy work for the cooler hours prevent heat illness; thirst comes late."}],steps:[{title:"Watch for early signs",detail:"In heat and cold, check yourself and your buddy regularly: thirst, headache, dizziness, strong shivering, clumsy hands or a change in behavior."},{title:"Stop work and protect",detail:"At the first sign, stop work: in the heat, move the person to the shade and loosen their clothing; in the cold, get them out of the wind and wet."},{title:"Confusion in the heat: cool at once",detail:"Confusion, strange behavior or collapse in the heat is heat stroke until proven otherwise: call 122 and cool at once, before transport; best is the body up to the neck in cold water.",critical:!0},{title:"Nothing to drink while confused",detail:"Give sips of water only to a fully alert person; give nothing to a confused person, because they may choke.",critical:!0},{title:"Cold casualty: dry, insulate, cover",detail:"Remove wet clothing, put on dry layers, insulate the person from the ground and cover the head and neck; rewarm gradually, never with direct fire.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 for danger signs",detail:"Shivering that stops, confusion or drowsiness in the cold is an emergency: call 122, move the person gently and keep them warm and still.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never give a drink to a confused heat casualty: they may choke, and drinking does not cool heat stroke.","Never delay cooling a heat stroke casualty for transport: cool first and at once while help is on its way.","Never rub frostbite or warm it over direct fire or a heater: numb skin burns and tears easily."],tips:["Plan heavy work for the cooler hours of the day when the mission allows it.","Drink water regularly during heavy work in the heat; do not wait until you feel thirsty.","On cold-weather duty, carry spare dry socks and a dry layer in a waterproof bag.","Dark urine is a sign that you need to drink more water."],activity:{prompt:"At a checkpoint on a hot afternoon, a colleague starts talking strangely and does not know where he is. His skin is still sweating. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Give him plenty of cold water and let him rest in the guard room",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a confused person gets nothing to drink, and rest alone does not cool heat stroke."},{text:"Call 122 and cool him at once with cold water, wet towels, ice packs and fanning",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: confusion in the heat is heat stroke even when the skin sweats; cooling at once saves his life."},{text:"Drive him straight to the hospital and cool him only when you arrive",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: cooling comes first and before transport; every minute of delay adds to the damage."},{text:"Let him sleep in the shade, since sweating shows it is not heat stroke",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the skin may still sweat in heat stroke; confusion is the danger sign."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A winter patrol returns to a mountain post in wind and rain. One soldier is soaked through, shivering hard and cannot hold his equipment properly. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Bring him inside, remove his wet clothes, dry him and cover him with dry layers and a blanket",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stop the heat loss first: shelter, dry clothing, insulation from the ground and a cover for the head and neck."},{text:"Rub his hands and feet hard to get the blood moving again",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: rubbing does not rewarm the body and can injure cold or frozen skin."},{text:"Give him an alcoholic drink to warm him from the inside",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: alcohol makes the body lose heat faster and weakens judgment."},{text:"Sit him right against the stove so he warms up as fast as possible",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: rewarm gradually; direct heat can burn numb skin."}]},{prompt:"Later his shivering stops, he becomes drowsy and gives confused answers. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Call 122, keep him lying down and warm, and move him gently",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: shivering that stops with confusion is severe hypothermia and an emergency; rough handling can harm the heart."},{text:"Give him a hot sweet drink to wake him up",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a drowsy, confused person gets nothing by mouth, because he may choke."},{text:"Walk him around the room to warm his body up",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: exercise and rough movement can harm a person with severe hypothermia; keep him still and warm."},{text:"Let him sleep, since the shivering has stopped",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: shivering that stops is a danger sign, not a sign of recovery."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): During a summer exercise, a field unit used the buddy system and planned its heavy work for the cooler morning hours. In the afternoon, one soldier noticed that his partner was answering slowly and walking unsteadily, although his skin was wet with sweat. He stopped work at once and called for help. The team leader called 122 while the others moved the soldier into the shade, removed his heavy equipment and cooled him with cold water, wet towels and fanning. They gave him nothing to drink while he was confused. By the time the ambulance arrived, he was thinking clearly again. In the after-action review, the unit agreed that the buddy check made the difference."},{id:11,code:"MIL-111",icon:"Ambulance",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Casualty evacuation (MEDEVAC)",subtitle:"Casualty evacuation and 9-Line",summary:"Safe evacuation and the 9-Line MEDEVAC request",blocks:[{body:"First aid is the first step, but rapid evacuation to the hospital saves lives."},{heading:"1. Carrying casualties",body:"Techniques:",points:["Two-person carry: one at the head end and one at the feet.","Fireman's carry (over the shoulder): by one person; fast.","Stretcher: by 4 people; the best option.","Improvised stretcher: 2 poles and a jacket or a blanket.","Suspected neck or back injury: do not move the casualty unless there is a risk of fire or explosion.","Move the head, neck and back together, in one line."]},{heading:"2. 9-Line MEDEVAC",body:"9 lines:",points:["Line 1: pickup location (coordinates / GPS).","Line 2: radio frequency and call sign.","Line 3: number of casualties by precedence (Urgent, Priority, Routine).","Line 4: special equipment (such as a hoist).","Line 5: number of casualties by type: L on a stretcher (litter), A able to walk (ambulatory).","Line 6: security at the pickup site: N no enemy, P possible enemy, E enemy present, X armed escort required.","Line 7: method of marking the site (panel, colored smoke, light).","Line 8: nationality and status of casualties (military, civilian, prisoner).","Line 9: CBRN contamination in wartime; or a description of the site terrain in peacetime."]},{heading:"3. Helicopter landing zone (LZ)",body:"For the helicopter:",points:["Size: a circle at least 35 m in diameter for a light helicopter (such as the UH-1), 50 m for the UH-60 and 80 m for cargo helicopters.","No wires, trees or tall obstacles; level, clear ground.","Indicate the wind direction (the helicopter lands into the wind).","At night: 4 lights in an inverted Y.","Remove dust and loose light objects from the site, because the rotor wash blows them into the air.","Stay 30 m away until you are signaled to approach."]},{heading:"4. Preparing the casualty for evacuation",body:"Before the casualty leaves, make sure they arrive alive:",points:["Treat life threats first: severe bleeding, the airway and breathing (MARCH).","Keep the casualty warm: one blanket under and one over them, even in mild weather, because blood loss makes the body cold.","Record what you found and did: the injuries, the treatment, the tourniquet time and any changes.","Keep the record and the casualty's ID with the casualty, not in someone else's pocket.","Secure the casualty firmly on the stretcher and keep checking breathing and bleeding during the move.","If the casualty is conscious, tell them what is happening and stay calm."]},{heading:"5. Civilian casualties: 122 and 115",body:"In peacetime, most casualties reach the hospital by ambulance:",points:["Call 122 for casualties, and also call 115 if there is fire, a collapse, a trapped person or hazardous materials.","Give the location first (area, street, a clear landmark), then what happened, how many are hurt or trapped, the dangers, and your name and phone number.","Do not hang up until you are told to.","Send someone to the main road to meet the ambulance and guide it in.","Keep the access route clear of vehicles and onlookers.","Helicopter evacuation of civilian casualties follows the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_medevac.mp4",title:"MEDEVAC",description:"Evacuation"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/mil_9line.png",title:"9-Line",description:"The form"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the minimum size of a landing zone (LZ) for a light helicopter (such as the UH-1)?",options:[{text:"10 m",correct:!1},{text:"20 m",correct:!1},{text:"35 m",correct:!0},{text:"5 m",correct:!1}],explanation:"A circle 35 m in diameter for a light helicopter; larger helicopters need a larger area (50 m for the UH-60)."},{question:"In the 9-Line, what is Line 1?",options:[{text:"Number of casualties by precedence",correct:!1},{text:"Pickup location (coordinates)",correct:!0},{text:"Security at the pickup site",correct:!1},{text:"Radio frequency and call sign",correct:!1}],explanation:"Line 1 is the pickup location, given as coordinates."},{question:"A casualty may have a neck or back injury. When may you move them?",options:[{text:"As soon as two people are available to carry them",correct:!1},{text:"Once they say they can feel their arms and legs",correct:!1},{text:"Only if there is a risk of fire or explosion",correct:!0},{text:"Whenever the helicopter landing zone is ready",correct:!1}],explanation:"With a suspected neck or back injury, do not move the casualty unless there is a risk of fire or explosion; if you must move them, move the head, neck and back together, in one line."},{question:"After a road crash, a civilian is bleeding heavily from the arm. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Stop the bleeding while someone else calls 122",correct:!0},{text:"Carry the casualty to your vehicle and head for the hospital",correct:!1},{text:"Fill in the 9-Line card before you touch the casualty",correct:!1},{text:"Clear a landing zone in case a helicopter is needed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life-threatening bleeding is treated at once; evacuation does not wait, but treatment comes first. Have someone else call 122 and give the location first."},{question:"When you call 122 for a casualty, what do you say first?",options:[{text:"The location: the area, the street and a clear landmark",correct:!0},{text:"Your name, your rank and the unit you belong to",correct:!1},{text:"The full story of how the accident happened",correct:!1},{text:"The casualty's age and the name of their family",correct:!1}],explanation:"The location comes first, so that help can be sent even if the call is cut off; then what happened, how many are hurt, the dangers, and your name and phone number."},{question:"On the way, a casualty with a tourniquet asks you to loosen it because it hurts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it tight, reassure him and tell the medical team when it went on",correct:!0},{text:"Loosen it a little every few minutes so that blood reaches the leg",correct:!1},{text:"Take it off completely as soon as the bleeding seems to have stopped",correct:!1},{text:"Loosen it a little and press on the wound with your hand instead",correct:!1}],explanation:"First aiders never loosen a tourniquet: the bleeding can start again. Reassure the casualty and pass on the time it was applied."}],steps:[{title:"Make the scene safe and treat first",detail:"Check for danger before you approach; then treat life threats first: severe bleeding, the airway and breathing.",critical:!0},{title:"Decide whether to move",detail:"With a suspected neck or back injury, do not move the casualty unless there is fire, explosion or another immediate danger; if you must, move the head, neck and back in line.",critical:!0},{title:"Request evacuation",detail:"Call 122 for civilian casualties, or send the 9-Line through your chain of command; give the location first."},{title:"Prepare the casualty",detail:"Keep the casualty warm, record the treatment and the tourniquet time, and secure them firmly on the stretcher."},{title:"Prepare the pickup point",detail:"Clear and mark the pickup point or helicopter landing zone, show the wind direction and keep everyone back."},{title:"Hand over",detail:"Brief the medical team on what happened, what you did and the times, hand over the record and account for everyone."}],warnings:["Never approach a helicopter until its crew signals you: the rotor and flying debris can kill.","Never loosen a tourniquet during transport, even if the casualty asks: the bleeding can start again.","Never leave a casualty alone while you wait for evacuation: their condition can change quickly."],tips:["Keep a printed 9-Line card with the radio and fill it in before you call.","Write the tourniquet time where the medical team will see it, for example on a card attached to the casualty.","Before the helicopter arrives, tie down tents, tarps and loose equipment near the landing zone.","Practise stretcher carries with the whole team, on rough ground too."],activity:{prompt:"On patrol near a village road, you come across a car crash. One driver is bleeding heavily from the thigh; the other is awake but has neck pain and says he cannot feel his legs properly. There is no fire or traffic at the scene. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Put both of them in your vehicle and drive them to the hospital at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving the neck-injury casualty without need can cause permanent damage, and the bleeding must be stopped first."},{text:"Stop the bleeding with pressure or a tourniquet, keep the second casualty still and call 122, giving the location first",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stop the life-threatening bleeding first, do not move a suspected spinal injury in a safe place, and call 122."},{text:"Sit the neck-pain casualty up in the shade and give both water until help comes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a suspected back injury stays still where it is, and a severe injury can lead to shock, when nothing is given by mouth."},{text:"Request a helicopter and wait for it before treating anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: treatment does not wait for transport; severe bleeding kills in a short time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a remote field camp, a worker has fallen from a water tank and cannot be taken out quickly by road. Your command arranges a helicopter and asks you to prepare the landing zone. Which site do you choose?",options:[{text:"A level, open area with no wires or trees, loose objects removed and the wind direction shown",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a landing zone must be level, free of obstacles and loose objects, and show the wind, because the helicopter lands into the wind."},{text:"The space right next to the tents, so the casualty does not have to be carried",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: rotor wash blows tents and loose objects into the air and into the rotor."},{text:"A shady spot under the trees, so the casualty stays out of the sun",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: trees are obstacles for the rotor; the landing zone must be open."},{text:"The flat road beside the power line, because it is the closest",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: wires are among the most dangerous obstacles for a helicopter."}]},{prompt:"The helicopter is coming in to land. A colleague wants to run toward it to wave it in and carry the stretcher under the rotor at once. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep everyone back until the crew signals, then approach with the stretcher as the crew directs",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stay back until you are signaled; the helicopter crew controls the approach."},{text:"Run forward with the stretcher as soon as the wheels touch the ground",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the rotor is still turning and the crew has not signaled; approaching early can kill."},{text:"Wave a jacket in the air over the landing zone to guide the pilot",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: loose items can be drawn into the rotor; the site was marked in advance."},{text:"Light a fire on the landing zone so the pilot can see the smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: fire on the landing zone is a danger; the site is marked with a panel, colored smoke or lights as planned."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): During a winter exercise, a soldier slipped on rocks and injured his leg and back. His team kept him still, stopped the bleeding with a tourniquet and wrote the time on a card attached to his jacket. Because snow had blocked the road, the team leader sent a 9-Line through the chain of command, giving the location first. Two soldiers cleared a level area away from the trees, tied down a tarp and marked the wind direction. When the helicopter landed, everyone waited until the crew signaled. At the handover, the team leader briefed the medics on the injury, the tourniquet time and the changes they had seen."},{id:12,code:"MIL-112",icon:"Search",accent:"#d97706",title:"Search and rescue (SAR)",subtitle:"Search and rescue operations",summary:"Operations to search for and rescue people",blocks:[{body:"During earthquakes, floods and explosions, people become trapped under rubble. Peshmerga are often the first to reach the scene."},{heading:"1. Search methods",body:"Patterns:",points:["Line search: in rows, at regular spacing.","Spiral: from the center outward.","Sector: the area is divided into sectors.","Search dogs (K9).","At set intervals (for example every 10 minutes), call for silence and listen.","Call out loudly to people and listen for a reply."]},{heading:"2. Under rubble",body:"Rescue:",points:["Locate the person: by listening and with search dogs.","Talk to the victim and reassure them.","Extract them slowly and carefully.","Watch out for the danger of a secondary collapse.","MARCH immediately for casualties.","Call 115 and 122."]},{heading:"3. Water rescue",body:"Floods and drowning:",points:["Do not enter the water if you have not been trained in rescue.","From the bank, reach out or throw something long: a rope, a branch, a long piece of clothing.","Put on a life jacket.","Human chains only in shallow, slow-moving water.","Once the person is out of the water, check their breathing. If they are not breathing normally: call 122 and, if you are trained, start CPR with breaths (drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most); if you are not trained or cannot give breaths, give chest compressions only. Anyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well.","15 cm of moving water can knock a person down; 30 cm can carry a car away."]},{heading:"4. Rescuer safety at a collapse",body:"A rescuer who becomes a casualty cannot help anyone:",points:["Report to the Civil Defense incident commander and work in the sector you are given.","Do not enter a damaged or unstable building unless you are trained, equipped and directed to.","Wear a helmet, gloves, boots and a dust mask; work in pairs and never alone.","Watch for gas, broken electrical cables, leaking water and further collapse; aftershocks can follow an earthquake.","If you smell gas: no flames, no switches and no phone near it; warn others, move away and report it to 115 from a safe place.","Know at all times who is inside the site; one person watches the structure and gives the warning to get out."]},{heading:"5. Helping the public at the scene",body:"Neighbors and volunteers arrive first and want to help:",points:["Keep onlookers off the rubble and away from damaged buildings.","Organize volunteers in teams under the direction of Civil Defense, with simple tasks outside the danger zone.","First bring out the people near the surface who are easy to reach; deep rescue is for trained teams.","Write down the names of the people found, where they were found and where they were taken.","Give families information only through the command post, so that rumors do not spread.","Keep the access roads open for rescue vehicles and ambulances."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_sar.mp4",title:"SAR",description:"Search and rescue"}],quiz:[{question:"When searching under rubble, what is done at set intervals (for example every 10 minutes)?",options:[{text:"Everyone runs away",correct:!1},{text:"Silence and listening",correct:!0},{text:"Everyone changes clothes",correct:!1},{text:"Everyone drinks water",correct:!1}],explanation:"All work stops and everyone falls silent, so that the victims' voices can be heard."},{question:"If someone is drowning and you have not been trained in rescue, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Jump straight into the water and pull them out by yourself",correct:!1},{text:"Reach out or throw something long to them from the bank",correct:!0},{text:"Only watch from the bank until someone else arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Run away and leave the scene",correct:!1}],explanation:"From the bank, reach out or throw something long to them and call 115; do not go into the water."},{question:"A person pulled from the water is not breathing normally, and you are trained. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Give chest compressions only, because breaths do not matter after drowning",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the person upside down to drain the water from the lungs",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for the person to cough up the water before doing anything",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122 and start CPR with breaths",correct:!0}],explanation:"If the person is not breathing normally, call 122 and, if you are trained, start CPR with breaths: drowning stops the breathing first, so breaths matter most. Anyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor."},{question:"During a rubble search, you smell gas. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop flames and switches nearby, warn others, move away and report to 115",correct:!0},{text:"Keep searching, but tell everyone to work faster before it gets worse",correct:!1},{text:"Go into the building yourself to find the gas valve and close it",correct:!1},{text:"Light a lighter to see where the gas is coming from",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gas under rubble can explode: no flames, switches or phones nearby; warn others, move away and report from a safe place. No one goes into the gas area for a valve."},{question:"Your unit arrives at a collapse where Civil Defense has set up a command post. What does your commander do first?",options:[{text:"Reports to the command post and receives a task and a sector",correct:!0},{text:"Starts a separate search in the most damaged street at once",correct:!1},{text:"Waits at the edge of the site until the operation is over",correct:!1},{text:"Takes command of the site because the unit has more people",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense leads the rescue; military units report to the command post, receive a sector and work within it."},{question:"What is the safest way to free a person trapped under light rubble?",options:[{text:"Remove the rubble by hand, slowly and in order, protecting the head and neck",correct:!0},{text:"Pull the person out by the arms as fast as possible so no time is lost",correct:!1},{text:"Lift the largest slab first with a vehicle, then look for the person",correct:!1},{text:"Call out to the person and wait for them to free themselves",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rubble is removed by hand, slowly and in order, so that it does not shift; protect the head, neck and back during the extraction."}],steps:[{title:"Size up and report",detail:"Look at the whole site from a safe place, then report to 115 and the incident commander: the location, the type of collapse, how many people may be trapped and the dangers.",critical:!0},{title:"Make the site safe",detail:"Keep out of unstable structures, watch for gas, electricity and further collapse, and follow the gas-leak rule if you smell gas.",critical:!0},{title:"Search systematically",detail:"Search in the pattern you are given, call out, stop for silence at set intervals and listen for replies or knocking."},{title:"Reach and reassure",detail:"Talk to the trapped person, tell them help is coming, and check for life threats with MARCH as soon as you can reach them."},{title:"Extract with care",detail:"Remove the rubble by hand, slowly and in order, with a trained team; protect the head, neck and back during the extraction.",critical:!0},{title:"Account and report",detail:"Count all rescuers, record who was found and where, and report to the command post before the team leaves the site."}],warnings:["Never enter a damaged building alone: a further collapse or an aftershock can trap the rescuer too.","Never use flames, lighters or switches where you smell gas: a spark can cause an explosion under the rubble.","Never pull a trapped person out by force: you can cause new injuries and bring the rubble down on them and on you."],tips:['Before the search starts, agree on simple signals for silence and for "get out now."',"Rotate teams regularly: tired rescuers make mistakes.","Keep a simple sketch of the site at the command post that shows which sectors have been searched.","For night work, carry a flashlight, a whistle and spare batteries."],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, your unit reaches a house that has partly collapsed. You hear knocking from under the rubble, and there is a strong smell of gas. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Light a lighter to look into the gaps under the rubble",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a flame where gas has collected can cause an explosion."},{text:"Keep everyone away from the gas, warn people, call 115 from a safe place and report the knocking and where it is",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the gas makes the site unsafe; Civil Defense needs both facts to plan a safe rescue."},{text:"Pull the large slab off quickly with a vehicle to reach the person",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving heavy rubble without a plan can bring a further collapse down on the trapped person."},{text:"Send two soldiers inside to find the gas valve and close it",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: no one goes into the area where gas has collected to reach a valve."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Villagers call your post: a man is standing on the roof of a car in fast-flowing flood water. Your unit has a rope, but no one is trained in water rescue. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 115 and throw the rope to the man from the bank while you stay out of the water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: reach or throw from the bank; untrained people do not enter fast-flowing water."},{text:"Drive the military truck into the water to reach the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving water can carry away even a large vehicle and the people in it."},{text:"Form a human chain into the fast-flowing water",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: human chains are only for shallow, slow-moving water."},{text:"Send your strongest swimmer to swim out to him",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a swimmer without rescue training and equipment can become a second victim."}]},{prompt:"The man is pulled to the bank. He is coughing, wet and cold, and says he is fine and wants to go home. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Keep him warm, call 122 and make sure a doctor checks him, even though he seems well",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: anyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, because problems can appear later."},{text:"Let him go home, since he can talk and walk",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a person who seems well after being in the water can still become seriously ill."},{text:"Turn him upside down to empty the water from his lungs",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this does not help and wastes time; check his breathing and keep him warm."},{text:"Give him a strong drink to warm him up and send him back to his car",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: he needs warmth and a medical check, not a return to the flood."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): After a strong earthquake, a unit reached a street where part of a two-story house had collapsed. The commander reported to the Civil Defense command post and was given a sector. One soldier was posted to watch the building, and the team agreed on a signal to get out. During a silence period, they heard a voice from under the stairs. They talked to the woman and reassured her while the Civil Defense team shored up the area. When they reached her, the team checked her for bleeding and kept her head and neck in line during the extraction. Before leaving, the commander counted all the rescuers and reported to the command post."},{id:13,code:"MIL-113",icon:"Flame",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire in military bases and ammunition depots",subtitle:"Fire safety in military bases",summary:"Fire prevention and safe evacuation in military bases and ammunition depots",blocks:[{body:"Fire in military bases is more dangerous, because weapons, ammunition and fuel are stored there. If a fire reaches ammunition or is close to it, there is no firefighting: get out, cordon off the area and call 115."},{heading:"1. Sources of fire",body:"On bases:",points:["Ammunition and explosives.","Gasoline and diesel.","Tent heaters.","Generators.","Smoking.","Faulty or overloaded electrical wiring."]},{heading:"2. Fire extinguishers",body:"Types (classes according to NFPA):",points:["ABC powder: for Classes A, B and C; not for D (metals) or K (cooking oil).","CO₂: for electrical and equipment fires (C) and liquids (B).","An ammunition fire cannot be put out with an extinguisher: get out, cordon off, call 115.","PASS, only when every go/no-go answer is yes (small, new fire; right extinguisher; exit behind you; alarm raised and 115 called; you are trained; no smoke or heat in your way): Pull (the pin), Aim (at the base of the fire), Squeeze, Sweep; if the fire does not go out at once, get out.","Place extinguishers near the exit doors.","Monthly visual inspection and annual maintenance."]},{heading:"3. Ammunition depot (explosives storage)",body:"Special rules:",points:["Prohibited: cigarettes, open flames, matches, lighters and cell phones.","Protect from heat and direct sunlight, according to the ammunition storage rules.","Anti-static clothing and gloves.","Fire detection devices and automatic sprinklers.","Only authorized personnel may enter.","A complete inventory: what, how much and where (to inform 115)."]},{heading:"4. Evacuation plan",body:"If a fire breaks out in an ammunition depot or near ammunition: do not fight the fire. Get out, cordon off the area and call 115.",points:["Sound the siren and alert everyone.","Get out quickly by a route away from the depot (at least 2 exit routes).","Evacuation and cordon area for a depot fire: the distance set by your command's explosives safety rules for that site. The ERG Guide 112 distance, 1600 m (1 mile) in all directions, is for explosives in transport (a truck trailer or rail car in a fire, or fire reaching the cargo); do not use it as the rule for every depot fire.","Assembly point: outside the evacuation area, upwind (the side the wind is coming from), behind a barrier, out of the line of sight and away from windows.","Headcount: everyone must be accounted for; give the names of missing persons to 115.","Call 115 (location, type and quantity of ammunition)."]},{heading:"5. Everyday fire prevention around stores",body:"Most fires start small and from ordinary causes:",points:["Keep the area around stores clear of dry grass, rubbish and empty packaging.","Store fuel, gas cylinders and other flammable materials apart from ammunition, as your command's storage rules require.","Keep heaters, generators, cooking and smoking areas away from stores, in the places your command has set.","Electrical work and repairs are done only by a qualified electrician; report damaged cables and overloaded sockets.","Hot work such as welding or cutting near stores is done only with the approval and fire watch set by your command.","Leave phones, lighters and matches at the entry point before you go in."]},{heading:"6. After a fire or explosion: stay out",body:"The danger does not end when the flames die down:",points:["Ammunition can still explode after the fire seems to be out.","Do not go back to the site to collect equipment, vehicles or ammunition.","Items thrown out by an explosion are dangerous: do not touch them, do not go closer and keep others away.","Report any item you see to your command and to 115.","Return only when the specialist teams and Civil Defense declare the area safe.","Through the authorities, warn nearby residents to keep away and to report items they find."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_fire_base.mp4",title:"Fire",description:"Military base"}],quiz:[{question:"What is prohibited in an ammunition depot?",options:[{text:"Recording the ammunition inventory",correct:!1},{text:"Cigarettes, open flames and cell phones",correct:!0},{text:"Talking with colleagues during the shift",correct:!1},{text:"Wearing anti-static clothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cigarettes, open flames and cell phones are prohibited, because they can cause a fire or an explosion."},{question:"Under ERG Guide 112, if a truck trailer carrying explosives is involved in a fire, how large must the isolation and evacuation area be?",options:[{text:"50 m around the trailer, until the fire is out",correct:!1},{text:"100 m in all directions, except for firefighters",correct:!1},{text:"1600 m (1 mile) in all directions, emergency responders included",correct:!0},{text:"800 m (half a mile) in all directions, while responders fight the fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to ERG Guide 112 (explosives in transport, Divisions 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5): if a truck trailer or rail car is involved in a fire, isolate and evacuate 1600 m (1 mile) in all directions, emergency responders included; if the fire reaches the cargo, do not fight it and let it burn. For a fire in an ammunition depot, use the distance in your command's explosives safety rules. The assembly point must be outside the evacuation area and upwind."},{question:"A fire breaks out in an ammunition depot. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Do not fight it: get out, cordon off the area and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Use a CO₂ extinguisher on the burning ammunition crates",correct:!1},{text:"Cool the crates with water while the others evacuate the base",correct:!1},{text:"Use ABC powder, because it covers Classes A, B and C",correct:!1}],explanation:"If a fire breaks out in an ammunition depot or near ammunition, do not fight it: get out, cordon off the area and call 115. An ammunition fire cannot be put out with an extinguisher."},{question:"A grass fire is moving toward an ammunition store. What do the guards do?",options:[{text:"Raise the alarm, evacuate away from the store and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Fight the grass fire next to the store until help arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the ammunition crates out before the fire reaches them",correct:!1},{text:"Close the store doors and stand guard at the entrance",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire moving toward ammunition is not fought: raise the alarm, evacuate by a route away from the store and call 115."},{question:"After an explosion at a store, you see a metal object lying in a field near the base. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Do not touch it, keep others away and report it",correct:!0},{text:"Pick it up carefully and take it back to the store",correct:!1},{text:"Cover it with soil so that no one steps on it",correct:!1},{text:"Move it to the roadside so the team can find it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Items thrown out by an explosion can still go off: do not touch or move them, keep others away and report to your command and 115."},{question:"Why is the assembly point for a store fire placed upwind and behind a barrier?",options:[{text:"To keep people out of the smoke and protected from blast and fragments",correct:!0},{text:"So that people can watch the fire and report on how it develops",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is closer to the vehicles and to the main gate",correct:!1},{text:"So that firefighters can find people quickly near the store",correct:!1}],explanation:"Upwind keeps people out of the smoke; a barrier and staying out of sight of the store protect them if there is an explosion."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"Sound the siren, shout a warning and make sure everyone on the site knows: fire near the ammunition store.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not fight it: get out",detail:"Do not fight a fire on or near ammunition; leave at once by the route that leads away from the store.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115",detail:"Call 115 from a safe place: the location, what is burning, and the type and quantity of ammunition from the inventory."},{title:"Cordon off and assemble",detail:"Keep everyone outside the distance set by your command's explosives safety rules; gather at the assembly point upwind, behind a barrier and out of sight of the store.",critical:!0},{title:"Account for everyone",detail:"Take a headcount; give 115 the name and last known location of anyone missing, and do not go back to search.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay out afterward",detail:"Do not return until the specialist teams declare the area safe; do not touch any item thrown out by an explosion, and report it.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never fight a fire that has reached ammunition or is close to it: an explosion can come without warning.","Never go back inside the cordon for equipment, vehicles or a missing person: tell 115 and wait.","Never touch or pick up an item thrown out by an explosion: it may still go off."],tips:["Know both exit routes and the assembly point before your first shift at the store.","Keep a copy of the ammunition inventory with the duty officer outside the store, so that it can be given to 115.","On every shift, check the area around the store for dry grass and rubbish.","Practise the evacuation and the headcount in drills, at night too."],activity:{prompt:"On guard duty, you see smoke rising from dry grass near the fence of the ammunition store. The wind is spreading the fire toward the store. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Raise the alarm, call 115, warn everyone and move people away from the store",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: guards do not fight a fire moving toward ammunition; the alarm, evacuation and 115 save lives."},{text:"Beat out the grass fire with branches right next to the store",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: if the fire reaches the store while you are beside it, an explosion can kill you; do not fight it there."},{text:"Go into the store and carry the crates out before the fire arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: no one enters a store threatened by fire; people matter more than any equipment."},{text:"Stay at your post until the duty officer arrives and gives an order",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: every minute counts; raise the alarm yourself at once."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The siren sounds. Your section reaches the usual assembly point, but you notice that it is downwind of the burning store and that the store can be seen from there. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Move the group to the alternative point upwind, behind a barrier and out of sight of the store, and report the change",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the assembly point must be outside the cordon, upwind, behind a barrier and out of the line of sight."},{text:"Stay where you are, because that is the point in the plan",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: if the point is unsafe today, move; the plan sets the safety principle, not just one place."},{text:"Move closer to the store to help the firefighters",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: no one approaches a fire at an ammunition store."},{text:"Go back to the vehicles to drive them out of the base",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: going back for vehicles puts lives at risk; leave them."}]},{prompt:"At the headcount, one soldier is missing. A colleague says he last saw him near the store. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Give his name and last known location to 115 and your command, and keep everyone at the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the specialist teams decide on any search; a second person who goes in can become a second victim."},{text:"Send two volunteers to search near the store quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: sending people near an ammunition fire puts more lives at risk."},{text:"Keep calling his phone and send his friend to look for him",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: sending anyone back puts another life at risk; report and wait."},{text:"Leave it, since he probably left by another route",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: anyone not accounted for must be reported to 115 at once."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): On a hot afternoon, a guard at a military base saw smoke rising from dry grass near the ammunition store. He raised the alarm at once and called 115, giving the location and what was stored there. The duty officer ordered everyone out by the route away from the store. The first group stopped at the usual assembly point, but a sergeant saw the wind carrying the smoke toward them and moved them to the alternative point behind a hill. At the headcount, one soldier was missing; his name was given to 115, and no one went back. He was later found safe at the other gate. After the review, the base cleared the grass around its stores."},{id:14,code:"MIL-114",icon:"Handshake",accent:"#059669",title:"Cooperation with Civil Defense",subtitle:"Civil-military cooperation (CIMIC)",summary:"Cooperation between the Peshmerga and Civil Defense (CIMIC)",blocks:[{body:"In disasters, the Peshmerga and Civil Defense work together."},{heading:"1. Peshmerga tasks",body:"In disasters:",points:["Search and rescue.","Security.","Logistics.","First aid.","Communications.","Reconstruction."]},{heading:"2. Coordination with Civil Defense (115)",body:"Communication:",points:["Call 115.","A shared communication channel.","Incident Command System (ICS).","Emergency Operations Center (EOC).","METHANE report.","Inside a city, command belongs to the civilian agencies."]},{heading:"3. Helping the public",body:"Humanitarian assistance:",points:["Food and water.","Tents for displaced people.","Field clinics.","Transporting people and supplies.","Protecting children.","Informing the public."]},{heading:"4. Community awareness",body:"Educating the public:",points:["Safety drills.","Mine awareness.","Schools.","Campaigns.","Children: simple teaching.","Radio and television."]},{heading:"5. Working on scene under Civil Defense command",body:"Military support is strongest when it fits into one plan:",points:["Move on your command's order, and let Civil Defense (115) know what your unit can offer and where it is going.","On arrival, the unit leader reports to the incident command post and lists the resources: people, vehicles, medics and engineers.","A liaison officer stays at the command post to pass on tasks and information.","Work only in the sector you are given, and do not enter a hazard zone without the incident commander's approval.","Report progress, problems and casualties to the command post regularly.","Leave only when the incident commander releases the unit, after a final headcount."]},{heading:"6. The METHANE report",body:"A short, fixed order so that nothing important is forgotten:",points:["M: major incident declared, or the alert you are raising.","E: exact location.","T: type of incident (collapse, flood, fire, crash).","H: hazards present and possible (gas, electricity, unstable buildings, water).","A: access routes and the best way in for responders.","N: number and type of casualties.","E: emergency services on scene and those still needed."]},{heading:"7. Communication and the public",body:"What you say and share in a disaster can help or harm:",points:["Use plain language on the shared channel, not military codes, so that every agency understands.","Do not post photos of victims or casualty numbers on social media.","Refer journalists and families to the official spokesperson and the command post.","Treat every affected person with respect and protect their dignity and privacy.","Give special care to children, older people, people with disabilities and anyone separated from their family.","Pass on only information from official sources, to stop rumors."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_cimic.mp4",title:"CIMIC",description:"Cooperation"}],quiz:[{question:"Inside a city during a disaster, who is in command?",options:[{text:"The military forces",correct:!1},{text:"Civil Defense",correct:!0},{text:"The police",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"The civilian agencies (Civil Defense) are in command, and the military forces provide support."},{question:"What is the role of the Peshmerga in a disaster?",options:[{text:"Only protecting their own bases",correct:!1},{text:"Staying away from the disaster area and leaving it to civilians",correct:!1},{text:"Taking full command of all civilian agencies, including Civil Defense",correct:!1},{text:"Rescue, security, logistics and first aid, in coordination with 115",correct:!0}],explanation:"The Peshmerga provide support in coordination with Civil Defense (115): rescue, security, logistics and first aid."},{question:"How do the Peshmerga coordinate with Civil Defense in a disaster?",options:[{text:"Run a separate operation and report only when it is over",correct:!1},{text:"Call 115, use a shared channel and work within the ICS",correct:!0},{text:"Use only their own military radio channels throughout",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until Civil Defense sends a written request for help",correct:!1}],explanation:"Coordination with Civil Defense means calling 115, using a shared communication channel and working within the Incident Command System (ICS) and the EOC; inside a city, command belongs to the civilian agencies."},{question:"Your unit reaches a disaster scene where Civil Defense is in command. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"The unit leader reports to the command post and receives a task",correct:!0},{text:"The unit starts work in the most damaged area it can see",correct:!1},{text:"The unit sets up its own command post beside the civilian one",correct:!1},{text:"The unit waits on its vehicles until someone comes to find it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reporting to the command post first lets the incident commander assign the unit safely and in coordination with 115."},{question:"Which way of speaking is best on the shared radio channel?",options:[{text:"Plain language that every agency understands",correct:!0},{text:"Military codes that only your unit understands",correct:!1},{text:"Long reports with every detail of the operation",correct:!1},{text:"Personal opinions about how other agencies work",correct:!1}],explanation:"On a shared channel, plain language prevents misunderstandings between agencies; keep messages short and clear."},{question:"A colleague wants to post photos of victims and the number of dead on social media. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Do not post them; leave figures to the official spokesperson",correct:!0},{text:"Post them, because it will bring help to the area faster",correct:!1},{text:"Post only the photos and leave out the number of the dead",correct:!1},{text:"Post only the number of the dead and leave out the photos",correct:!1}],explanation:"Photos of victims and unconfirmed figures hurt families and spread rumors; figures come only from the official spokesperson."}],steps:[{title:"Move on orders and inform",detail:"Move on your command's order and make sure Civil Defense (115) knows what your unit can offer and where it is going."},{title:"Report to the command post",detail:"On arrival, the unit leader reports to the incident command post and lists the people and resources available."},{title:"Receive a task and a sector",detail:"Take the task from the incident commander, agree on the shared channel and appoint a liaison officer."},{title:"Work safely within your sector",detail:"Carry out the assigned task within your sector; never enter a hazard zone without the incident commander's approval.",critical:!0},{title:"Report regularly",detail:"Send short reports to the command post, and use METHANE for new events and casualties."},{title:"Stand down in order",detail:"Leave only when the incident commander releases the unit, after a headcount and a handover of what remains."}],warnings:["Never start a separate rescue operation without informing the command post: two uncoordinated teams can endanger each other and the victims.","Never enter a hazard zone (gas, collapse, flood) without the incident commander's approval, even to help.","Never share photos of victims or unconfirmed figures: it hurts families and spreads rumors."],tips:["Keep an up-to-date list of your unit's people, vehicles and skills, ready to hand to the command post.","Hold joint drills with Civil Defense, so that the teams know each other before a disaster.","Agree in advance on the shared radio channel and on plain-language terms.","Wear clear identification so that civilians and other agencies know who you are."],activity:{prompt:"A flood hits a town. Civil Defense has set up its command post at the school. Your unit arrives with trucks and a full platoon. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Your commander reports to the command post, lists the trucks and soldiers and receives a sector",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: reporting in lets the incident commander use your trucks where they are most needed and keeps everyone safe."},{text:"Start evacuating the most flooded street at once, without telling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an uncoordinated operation can send people into danger or repeat work already done."},{text:"Wait at the base until Civil Defense sends a written request",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: once your command has agreed, you report to the command post; waiting for a letter loses time."},{text:"Take command of the operation because your unit has more people",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: inside a city, command belongs to the civilian agencies; the military supports."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"An earthquake has damaged several buildings in a town. Your company is ordered to support. As you arrive, residents run to you asking for help at three different houses. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Send the commander to the command post at once, keep the unit together nearby and pass on the residents' reports",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the command post knows where the teams and dangers are; your reports help it decide."},{text:"Split the company into three groups and send one to each house",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without a plan, groups may enter unsafe buildings and repeat other teams' work."},{text:"Tell the residents to wait until the operation is organized and ignore their reports",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: residents' information is valuable; pass it on to the command post."},{text:"Go into the nearest damaged house yourself to see what is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: entering a damaged building without assessment and approval can cost lives."}]},{prompt:"Later, a journalist asks you how many people have died and wants to photograph the victims you have brought out. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Refer him politely to the official spokesperson and do not allow photos of the victims",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only the official spokesperson gives figures; the victims' dignity must be protected."},{text:"Give him the number you heard from colleagues",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: unconfirmed numbers spread rumors and can hurt families."},{text:"Let him take photos, since the public has a right to know",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the public is informed through official channels, not through photos of victims."},{text:"Post the information on your own social media page so people know sooner",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: private posting bypasses the official channel and can hurt families."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): After heavy rain, a river flooded part of a town. Civil Defense set up its command post at a school. A military unit arrived with trucks; its commander reported to the command post and was given the task of moving families from two streets to a shelter. A liaison officer stayed at the command post, and the unit used the shared channel in plain language. When a wall began to crack, the unit reported it at once, and the incident commander moved the work to a safer street. When a journalist asked for casualty figures, the soldiers referred him to the official spokesperson. At the end, the unit left only after a headcount and the incident commander's release."},{id:15,code:"MIL-115",icon:"Megaphone",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Public awareness and community information",subtitle:"Community safety education",summary:"The role of the Peshmerga in educating the public about safety and civil defense",blocks:[{body:"The Peshmerga's duty is not only combat. Warning the public about hazards saves lives."},{heading:"1. Mine awareness",body:"Educating the public:",points:["Children: do not touch it, and tell an adult!","Farmers and builders: ask the authorities before digging; a place without warning signs is not necessarily safe.","Shepherds: do not graze livestock in mined areas.","Teach the signs of mines, and that they must never be removed.","If you see a mine or a strange object: do not go closer, keep others away and report it; in an emergency call 115.","In an area that may be mined: stop, stay still and do not move your feet, call for help and wait.","School lessons."]},{heading:"2. Earthquakes",body:"Teaching:",points:["Drop, Cover, Hold On: under a sturdy table, away from windows.","Inspection of buildings.","Emergency bag: water (4 liters per person per day, for at least 3 days).","Assembly point.","Family emergency plan.","Regular drills on the plan."]},{heading:"3. Fire and the home",body:"Safety:",points:["Gas heaters: good ventilation (risk of CO gas).","Electricity: do not overload sockets.","A fire extinguisher in every home.","Smoke alarm.","Remember the number 115.","2 exit routes."]},{heading:"4. First aid",body:"Basics:",points:["CPR: 30:2, 100-120 compressions per minute, depth 5-6 cm; if you are not trained, chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR).","Bleeding: firm, direct pressure.","Fractures: do not move it; immobilize it.","Burns: 20 minutes of cool running water.","Poisoning: call 122 and keep the container; do not make the person vomit and give nothing to eat or drink.","Write 115 and 122 on the wall."]},{heading:"5. Organizing campaigns",body:"How:",points:["Plan: topic + objective + area.","Team: Peshmerga + Civil Defense (115) + health services.","Venues: schools, mosques, villages.","Media: posters, videos, social media.","Target groups: children, women, officials.","Evaluation after the campaign."]},{heading:"6. How to speak to the public",body:"A good message is short, clear and practised:",points:["Choose a few key actions for each session, not a long list of facts.","Use the local language and simple words; avoid military terms.",'Show and practise: let people try "Drop, Cover, Hold On" or a call to 115.',"Adapt the message to the audience: children, older people, people with disabilities, farmers and shepherds.","Check understanding with questions at the end, and repeat the key actions.","Never bring real explosive items or ammunition to a session; use pictures."]},{heading:"7. Only correct and approved messages",body:"Wrong advice can kill, even when it is given with good intentions:",points:["Use only messages agreed with Civil Defense and the competent authority.","Give the right numbers: 115 Civil Defense, 122 ambulance, 104 police.",'Earthquakes: Drop, Cover, Hold On; never teach the doorway or the "triangle of life."',"Mines and strange objects: do not touch, do not go closer, keep others away and report; never teach the public to retrace their footsteps, probe the ground or mark objects.","Do not describe how mines or other devices work; it is not needed to stay safe.","If you do not know an answer, say so, write the question down and refer it to Civil Defense."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_awareness.mp4",title:"Awareness",description:"Education"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/mil_campaign.png",title:"Campaign",description:"Organization"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first lesson for children about mines?",options:[{text:"Pick it up carefully and give it to an adult",correct:!1},{text:"Do not touch it, and tell an adult!",correct:!0},{text:"Go looking for them",correct:!1},{text:"Throw stones at it from far away",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not touch it, move away and tell an adult; the adult calls 115."},{question:"In every home, what should be written on the wall?",options:[{text:"The name of the homeowner",correct:!1},{text:"115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"The color of the wall",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"The emergency numbers: 115 (Civil Defense) and 122 (Ambulance)."},{question:"Where does an awareness campaign reach the most people?",options:[{text:"Only inside the barracks",correct:!1},{text:"In schools, mosques and villages",correct:!0},{text:"In a remote place where nobody lives",correct:!1},{text:"Through a secret letter to one person",correct:!1}],explanation:"A campaign held where people gather (schools, mosques, villages) reaches the most people."},{question:"Which earthquake message is correct for the public?",options:[{text:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table, away from windows",correct:!0},{text:"Stand in a doorway until the shaking has completely stopped",correct:!1},{text:'Lie down next to a large piece of furniture ("triangle of life")',correct:!1},{text:"Run outside down the stairs as soon as the shaking starts",correct:!1}],explanation:'Drop, Cover, Hold On under sturdy furniture, away from windows, is the approved message; the doorway and the "triangle of life" are unsafe myths.'},{question:"During a session, a child asks you how a mine explodes. How do you answer?",options:[{text:"Simply: never touch it, move away and tell an adult",correct:!0},{text:"Explain in detail how a mine works so that he understands",correct:!1},{text:"Draw the inside of a mine on the board for the class",correct:!1},{text:"Promise to bring a real mine next time to show him",correct:!1}],explanation:"How devices work is not needed to stay safe and can make children curious; repeat the safe behavior: do not touch, move away, tell an adult."},{question:"A villager asks about a local safety rule that you are not sure about. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Say you are not sure, note the question and refer it to Civil Defense",correct:!0},{text:"Give the answer you think is most likely, so that you look confident",correct:!1},{text:"Tell him that there are no rules on that in this region",correct:!1},{text:"Change the subject so that the session can go on",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never guess local rules, numbers or channels; refer the question to Civil Defense and the competent authority."}],steps:[{title:"Plan with Civil Defense",detail:"Before the session, agree with Civil Defense on the topic, the objective, the audience and the approved messages."},{title:"Prepare simple materials",detail:"Prepare pictures, posters or short videos in the local language, with a few key actions."},{title:"Teach only approved actions",detail:"Speak simply, show each action and let people practise it; give only approved messages.",critical:!0},{title:"Check understanding",detail:"Ask questions at the end and correct any wrong idea at once, especially about mines and gas.",critical:!0},{title:"Leave the numbers",detail:"Make sure everyone knows 115 and 122 and how to report a strange object."},{title:"Evaluate and report",detail:"Record what was taught, who attended and the questions asked, and share them with Civil Defense to improve the next campaign."}],warnings:["Never bring real explosive items or ammunition to an awareness session: they are dangerous and encourage people to handle such objects.","Never teach unsafe myths, such as standing in a doorway during an earthquake or retracing footsteps in a minefield: they cost lives.","Never invent local rules, numbers or reporting channels: refer questions to Civil Defense."],tips:["Work with teachers, village leaders and mosque imams; people trust familiar voices.","For children, keep sessions short and use stories, pictures and games.","End every session by repeating the same three key actions.","Leave a poster with 115 and 122 where people gather."],activity:{prompt:"At a village session, a farmer says he found a rusty shell in his field last week and moved it to the side of the road. He asks what he should do now.",options:[{text:"Tell him not to touch it again, to keep people away from it and to report it; you also report it through your command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: do not touch, keep away and report; the specialist teams deal with it."},{text:"Offer to pick it up yourself and take it back to the base",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: no one except specialist teams moves a suspected explosive item."},{text:"Tell him to bury it so that children will not find it",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving or burying it again is dangerous and hides it from the specialist teams."},{text:"Tell him it is safe now, because it did not explode when he moved it",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an item that did not explode once can explode the next time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are asked to give a session on mines to a primary school class. How do you plan it?",options:[{text:"A short session with the teachers: pictures and one clear message: do not touch, move away, tell an adult",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: children remember a short, clear message with pictures and play; the teachers repeat it later."},{text:"Show the children a real defused mine so that they can recognize one",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: real items are never brought to sessions, and they encourage children to handle objects."},{text:"Explain the types of mines and how they work, so that the children understand the danger",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: how devices work is not needed and can make children curious; teach safe behavior."},{text:"A long lecture with many facts and figures about mine victims",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: children remember short messages and practice, not long lectures."}]},{prompt:"After the session, a boy tells you that his older brother collects metal pieces from a field near the village to sell them. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Tell the teacher and the family, and report the field to Civil Defense through your command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the danger is real; the adults and the authorities must act, and the field must be reported."},{text:"Ask the boy to bring one of the pieces to school so that you can check it",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a child must never carry such objects; this puts lives at risk."},{text:"Go with the boy to the field to see it for yourself",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: entering a suspected area without specialist teams is dangerous, and taking a child there is even more so."},{text:"Leave it, since it is the family's business",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a suspected hazard near a village must be reported to protect everyone."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A Civil Defense team and a military unit planned an awareness day in a village. They agreed on three messages: Drop, Cover, Hold On; do not touch strange objects, keep away and report; and the numbers 115 and 122. Teachers helped with the children's session, using pictures and a short game. At the mosque, the team talked with farmers and shepherds and let them practise a call to 115, giving the location first. When a farmer asked about a local permit rule, the officer wrote the question down and promised an answer from Civil Defense instead of guessing. At the end, everyone repeated the three messages."},{id:16,code:"MIL-116",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers: complete training",subtitle:"Fire extinguisher — complete training (PASS)",summary:"Detailed knowledge of all extinguisher types, fire classes and the PASS procedure",blocks:[{body:"The fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment for putting out small fires in their early stage. In barracks and military bases, every Peshmerga must be able to use an extinguisher quickly and correctly. Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds, and the exact discharge time is on the label, so they must be used quickly and correctly."},{heading:"1. Fire classes according to NFPA",body:"Under the NFPA system, fires are divided into 5 classes. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. The classes:",points:["Class A (ordinary solids): wood, paper, fabric, tents. Extinguishers: water, ABC powder, foam.","Class B (flammable liquids and gases): gasoline, kerosene, diesel, oils. Liquids: CO₂, ABC powder, foam. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop. (Never use water!)","Class C (electrical): generators, panels, switches. Extinguishers: CO₂, clean agent, ABC powder. (Do not use water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking, as on some water-mist units!)","Class D (metals): magnesium, aluminum. Extinguisher: only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal, not ABC. (Water can cause an explosion!)","Class K (kitchen): cooking oils. Extinguisher: wet chemical, not ABC. (Water spreads the burning oil!)"]},{heading:"2. Types of extinguishers",body:"Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols. Classes below are in NFPA letters:",points:["Water extinguisher: all red, no band; for Class A only.","CO₂ extinguisher: black band; for Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). Short range; not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. Hold only the handle, never the horn: it gets extremely cold and can freeze your hand! In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.","ABC (dry powder) extinguisher: blue band; for Classes A, B and C; not for D and K. The most common type.","Foam extinguisher: cream band; for Classes A and B. Do not use it on electrical fires!","Clean agent extinguisher: its rating depends on the model, so read the label (often B and C in NFPA letters); for sensitive equipment.","Wet chemical extinguisher: yellow band; for Class K (NFPA K = EN F). Class D extinguisher: its agent must be listed on the label for the metal in use."]},{heading:"3. The PASS procedure",body:"Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes): the fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket; the extinguisher is the right type; your exit is behind you; the alarm has been raised and 115 called; you are trained; no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, the extinguisher empties or smoke and heat increase, stop, get out and close the door. Life comes before property. PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers; Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions:",points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.","A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the flames!","S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handles firmly.","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side until the fire is out."]},{heading:"4. Monthly extinguisher inspection",body:"Inspect extinguishers visually every month (NFPA 10):",points:["Internal pressure: if there is a gauge, its needle must be in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.","Safety pin and its tamper seal: must be in place.","Hose/nozzle: must not be cracked or blocked.","Extinguisher body: must not be rusted or damaged.","Keep these separate: inspection and maintenance once a year by a qualified technician; recharge after any use, even a short one; every 6 years, stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside; hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder) every 5 years for water, foam, CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers, and every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. A test is not a replacement. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed."]},{heading:"5. Special hazards on military bases",body:"Military bases are sensitive locations:",points:["Fuel and gasoline: the fuel depot; Class B.","Ammunition depots and explosives: risk of explosion; do not fight the fire! Move away to the distance set by your command's explosives safety rules for that site, cordon off the area and call 115. The ERG Guide 112 distance, 1,600 m (1 mile) in all directions, is for explosives in transport (a vehicle carrying explosives in a fire, or fire reaching the cargo).","Generators and electricity: Class C; CO₂, clean agent or ABC powder; if you can, cut off the power first.","Tents and containers: dry materials; Class A.","Place extinguishers near doors; the travel distance to an extinguisher must not exceed 23 m for Class A, and 9-15 m for Class B (NFPA 10)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_fire_ext.mp4",title:"Fire extinguishers",description:"The PASS procedure for Peshmerga"}],quiz:[{question:"In NFPA letters, which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher rated for?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ is suitable for flammable liquids (B) and electrical equipment (C). Under EN 2, C means gas fires, and CO₂ is not rated for them."},{question:"In the PASS procedure, where should the extinguisher nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the top of the flames",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Away from the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire."},{question:"Why is water not poured on an oil fire (Class K)?",options:[{text:"It only cools the pot and does not reach the flames",correct:!1},{text:"Water spreads the burning oil and causes an explosion",correct:!0},{text:"The oil absorbs the water",correct:!1},{text:"Water reacts with the hot oil and turns into a toxic gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water on hot oil instantly turns to steam and spreads the burning oil (flash fire)."},{question:"Which situation means you must not try to fight a fire with an extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Smoke is building up between you and the only exit",correct:!0},{text:"The fire is small and has just started in a wastebasket",correct:!1},{text:"You have the right extinguisher and have been trained",correct:!1},{text:"The alarm has been raised and 115 has been called",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every go/no-go answer must be yes. Smoke or heat between you and the exit means no: get out, close the door and wait for 115."},{question:"You used an extinguisher for a few seconds and the gauge needle is still in the green. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Send it for recharge, because it must be recharged after any use",correct:!0},{text:"Hang it back on the wall, because the needle is still in the green",correct:!1},{text:"Shake it well and keep it for the next small fire",correct:!1},{text:"Take out the pin so that you can use it faster next time",correct:!1}],explanation:"An extinguisher is recharged after any use, even a short one; a partly used unit may fail when it is needed."},{question:"Gas is burning from a cylinder valve that cannot be reached safely. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Do not put out the flame: get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Put the flame out with a CO₂ extinguisher, then leave the room",correct:!1},{text:"Spray water on the flame until it goes out completely",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the cylinder with a wet blanket to smother the flame",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode. Shut off the gas only if it is safe; otherwise get out, raise the alarm and call 115."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:"Shout a warning, sound the fire alarm and make sure 115 has been called before you fight any fire.",critical:!0},{title:"Go/no-go check",detail:"Fight the fire only if every answer is yes: small and just starting, the right extinguisher, the exit behind you, the alarm raised and 115 called, you are trained, and no smoke or heat blocks your way.",critical:!0},{title:"Choose by the label",detail:"Read the label and its fire class symbols, not the color; for live electrical equipment, cut the power if you can do so safely."},{title:"PASS from the label distance",detail:"Start from the distance shown on the label: Pull the safety pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle and Sweep from side to side."},{title:"If it does not go out, get out",detail:"If the fire does not go out at once, the extinguisher empties or smoke and heat increase, stop, get out and close the door behind you.",critical:!0},{title:"After use",detail:"Watch the area from a safe place until 115 arrives, report the use and send the extinguisher for recharge, even after a short use."}],warnings:["Never use water or foam on live electrical equipment unless the label shows a rating for it: you can get an electric shock.","Never pour water on burning cooking oil: it turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out.","Never put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode."],tips:["In every building where you work, know where the extinguishers are on your way out.","During the monthly check, look at the pressure gauge, the safety pin and tamper seal, the hose and the body.","Practise PASS with training extinguishers under an instructor.","Never hang a used extinguisher back on the wall; send it for recharge."],activity:{prompt:"A small fire starts at the base of a generator beside a tent. The alarm has been raised and 115 called, the exit is behind you and you are trained. Next to you are a water extinguisher and a CO₂ extinguisher. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Cut the power if you can do so safely, use the CO₂ extinguisher aimed at the base, and get out if the fire does not go out at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a generator is live electrical equipment with fuel (classes C and B in NFPA letters); CO₂ suits both, and the go/no-go rule still applies."},{text:"Use the water extinguisher, because it holds more agent",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water on live electrical equipment can give you an electric shock and spreads burning fuel."},{text:"Throw a bucket of water over the generator",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the same danger as the water extinguisher: electric shock and spreading fuel."},{text:"Hold the CO₂ extinguisher by its horn to aim more accurately",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the horn gets extremely cold and can freeze your hand; hold only the handle."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the base kitchen, oil in a large pot catches fire. The cook shouts for help. There is a wet chemical extinguisher on the wall and a lid on the counter. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Turn off the heat if it is safe, slide the lid over the pot or use the wet chemical extinguisher as its label says; never water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: turning off the heat and covering the pot starve the fire of oxygen; wet chemical is the agent for cooking oil (NFPA K = EN F)."},{text:"Pour water over the pot to cool it quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water on burning oil turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out."},{text:"Use the ABC powder extinguisher, because it is the most common type",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: ABC powder is not for cooking-oil fires (class K/F)."},{text:"Carry the burning pot outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: carrying it spills burning oil on you and on the floor."}]},{prompt:"Before you can act, the flames reach the hood and the cupboards, and smoke fills the kitchen. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, close the kitchen door behind you and make sure 115 has been called",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fire is no longer small and smoke blocks the way; the go/no-go answer is now no."},{text:"Try again with a second extinguisher from the corridor",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: once the fire has spread, fighting it puts your life at risk; leave it to 115."},{text:"Open the windows and stay to see whether the fire goes out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staying in smoke is deadly, and fresh air can feed the fire."},{text:"Go back to the stove to turn off the gas",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the gas is shut off only if it is safe to reach; with smoke and flames, get out and tell 115 where the gas valve is."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): At night, a soldier on duty saw a small fire in a wastebasket in an office tent. He shouted a warning, and a colleague sounded the alarm and called 115. The soldier went through the go/no-go questions: the fire was small and just starting, the ABC powder extinguisher suited it, the exit was behind him and he had been trained. He started from the distance shown on the label and used PASS, and the fire went out at once. He stayed at the doorway to watch until the 115 team arrived and checked the tent. The next morning, the used extinguisher was sent for recharge and a full one was put in its place."},{id:17,code:"MIL-117",icon:"Heart",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid and CPR",subtitle:"First aid and CPR — complete training",summary:"Complete training in first aid, cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and emergency response on military bases",blocks:[{body:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or sick person before medical teams arrive. On military bases the risk of injury is very high, from combat and blast wounds to electric shock and falls. Every Peshmerga must know how to perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect the casualty until the ambulance (122) arrives."},{heading:"1. CPR: cardiopulmonary resuscitation (in detail)",body:"CPR is a life-saving procedure for when a person's heart or breathing stops:",points:["1. Scene safety: make sure the casualty's location is safe (no explosives, fire or gas?).",'2. Responsiveness: tap their shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right? Can you hear me?"',"3. Call 122: if there is no response and no normal breathing (take less than 10 seconds to check), call 122 immediately and get an AED.","4. Chest compressions: 30 compressions in the center of the chest; depth: 5-6 cm, rate: 100-120 compressions per minute.","5. Rescue breaths: tilt the head back and lift the chin, pinch the nose and give 2 breaths of 1 second each. If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping.","6. Continue (30:2): until the ambulance (122) arrives or the casualty is breathing normally.","7. AED: as soon as it arrives, switch it on, attach the pads to the chest and follow the device's voice prompts."]},{heading:"2. Bleeding control",body:"Severe bleeding can kill a person in a short time:",points:["Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it: put another cloth on top.","Wound packing: for deep wounds in the neck, armpit or groin, pack the wound with gauze and apply pressure.","Tourniquet: use it early for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, 5-7 cm above the wound; write down the time and never loosen it.","Impaled object: if metal, glass or blast fragments are lodged in the wound, do not pull them out! Stabilize the area around the object."]},{heading:"3. Burn treatment",body:"Burns in the field and on bases:",points:["Thermal burns: pour cool running water over the burned area for 20 minutes (not ice).","Chemical burns: first gently brush off any dry powder, then rinse with plenty of water for 20 minutes.","Electrical burns: first cut off the power! Then treat as a thermal burn, and call 122.","Do not burst burn blisters; do not use oil, creams or toothpaste."]},{heading:"4. Fractures and spinal injuries",body:"After a fall:",points:["Splinting: do not move the injured limb; immobilize it with a piece of wood.","Spinal protection: if someone has fallen from a height, do not move them; keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe (fire, traffic, collapse, gas, rising water), keeping the head, neck and back in line. If they need CPR, lay them on their back on a firm surface. Do not put them on their side (recovery position).","Cooling: wrap ice in a cloth and place it on the painful area (not directly on the skin)."]},{heading:"5. Electric shock and rescue",body:"Electricity can stop the heart:",points:["Cut off the power immediately! Do not touch the casualty until the power has been cut off.","If you cannot cut it off and it is low voltage: use a dry piece of wood or plastic to separate the wire from the casualty. Do not approach high voltage, and call 115.","Everyone who has had an electric shock must go to the hospital (122)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/mil_cpr.mp4",title:"CPR and first aid",description:"CPR training for Peshmerga"}],quiz:[{question:"In CPR, how many chest compressions should be given per minute?",options:[{text:"50-70",correct:!1},{text:"80-90",correct:!1},{text:"100-120",correct:!0},{text:"130-160",correct:!1}],explanation:"100-120 compressions per minute."},{question:"If a sharp object is lodged in a wound, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Pull it out",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Do not touch it; leave the casualty and go",correct:!1},{text:"Wash it with water and pull it out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pulling the object out will increase the bleeding; stabilize the area around it and call 122."},{question:"If someone has had an electric shock, what is the first action?",options:[{text:"Take hold of their hand",correct:!1},{text:"Start CPR",correct:!1},{text:"Cut off the power",correct:!0},{text:"Pour water on them",correct:!1}],explanation:"The power must be cut off first."},{question:"A colleague collapses and is only gasping. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Treat it as cardiac arrest: call 122, get the AED and start CPR",correct:!0},{text:"Treat it as a simple faint: raise his legs and wait for him to wake",correct:!1},{text:"Put him in the recovery position and keep checking his breathing",correct:!1},{text:"Give rescue breaths only, because he is still breathing a little",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gasping is not normal breathing. Call 122, send someone for the AED and start compressions at once."},{question:"You are not trained in rescue breaths (or do not want to give them). What do you do for an adult in cardiac arrest?",options:[{text:"Continuous chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR)",correct:!0},{text:"Nothing until someone trained arrives at the scene",correct:!1},{text:"Rescue breaths only, without any chest compressions",correct:!1},{text:"A few compressions, then wait to see if he recovers",correct:!1}],explanation:"For adults, untrained or unwilling rescuers give chest compressions only: hard, fast and without stopping until the AED or the ambulance arrives."},{question:"A soldier has fallen from a vehicle roof. He is unconscious but breathing normally, and the area is safe. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep him still as he was found, call 122 and keep checking his breathing",correct:!0},{text:"Turn him onto his side in the recovery position straight away",correct:!1},{text:"Prop his back against a wheel so that he can breathe more easily",correct:!1},{text:"Carry him into the shade so that he does not get too hot",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a fall, the neck or back may be injured: do not move him unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed, and do not use the recovery position. Call 122 and keep checking his breathing."}],steps:[{title:"Check the scene",detail:"Make sure the place is safe: no explosives, fire, gas, live electricity or traffic. Do not become a second casualty.",critical:!0},{title:"Check response and breathing",detail:"Tap the shoulders and shout; look for normal breathing for less than 10 seconds. Gasping is not normal breathing.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 and get an AED",detail:"Call 122 or have someone call, and send someone for an AED and the first aid kit.",critical:!0},{title:"Push hard and fast",detail:"Compress the center of the chest 5-6 cm deep at 100-120 per minute and let the chest come back up fully; 30:2 if you are trained and willing, otherwise compressions only.",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED as soon as it arrives",detail:"Switch it on, attach the pads to the chest and follow its voice prompts; keep pauses in compressions as short as possible.",critical:!0},{title:"Continue or use the recovery position",detail:"Continue until the ambulance team takes over or the person breathes normally; if they breathe normally and no neck, back, hip or pelvis injury is suspected, use the recovery position and keep checking them."}],warnings:["Never pause chest compressions for longer than needed: without them, no blood reaches the brain.","Never move a casualty after a fall or a blast unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed: you can injure the spine.","Never loosen a tourniquet or remove a dressing soaked with blood: put more dressings on top and keep up the pressure."],tips:["Change the person doing compressions regularly, with as short a pause as possible: compressions weaken as you tire.","Know where the AED and the first aid kits are on your base.","Carry your tourniquet in the same place every time, where either hand can reach it.","Practise CPR on a manikin regularly; skills fade without practice."],activity:{prompt:"During physical training, a soldier collapses. He does not respond and is taking slow, noisy, irregular gasps. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 122, send someone for the AED and start chest compressions at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gasping is not normal breathing; it is a sign of cardiac arrest."},{text:"Wait to see whether the gasping turns into normal breathing",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: every minute without compressions lowers the chance of survival."},{text:"Put him in the recovery position because he is still breathing",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: gasping is not normal breathing; the recovery position is only for normal breathing."},{text:"Splash water on his face and give him something to drink",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an unconscious person gets nothing by mouth, and water does not treat cardiac arrest."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A vehicle accident on base: a soldier is conscious, but blood is spurting from a deep wound in his thigh. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Apply a tourniquet 5-7 cm above the wound, note the time and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life-threatening bleeding from a limb needs a tourniquet early; the time helps the hospital."},{text:"Raise his leg and press on a pressure point at the top of the thigh",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: elevation and pressure points are no longer taught; they do not reliably stop severe bleeding."},{text:"Remove the soaked dressing to see how deep the wound is",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: removing a soaked dressing restarts the bleeding; put more dressings on top."},{text:"Wait for the ambulance and keep him talking",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: severe bleeding kills in a short time; act at once."}]},{prompt:"The bleeding has stopped. Soon after, he becomes pale, cold and sweaty and his thinking is confused. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Keep him lying on his back and warm, give nothing by mouth and keep checking him until 122 arrives",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: these are signs of shock; lying down, warmth and nothing by mouth are the first aid."},{text:"Loosen the tourniquet so that blood can return to the leg",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: first aiders never loosen a tourniquet; the bleeding can start again."},{text:"Give him water because he is losing fluid",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a person in shock gets nothing to eat or drink."},{text:"Sit him up and help him walk to the vehicle",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a person in shock stays lying down; sitting up or walking can make him collapse."}]}],realCase:"Practical case (training example): During a morning run at a base, a soldier collapsed and did not respond. His colleague saw that he was only gasping, shouted for help and started chest compressions at once, while a second soldier called 122 and a third ran for the AED. When the AED arrived, they switched it on, attached the pads and followed the voice prompts; it advised a shock, and they restarted compressions straight after it. The team changed compressors regularly, with very short pauses. When the ambulance team arrived, they took over care. Later, the unit added more AED training to its program."}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
